window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert12-phy-em-waves","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Electromagnetic Waves · Practice","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","subject":"Physics","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":1,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A radio receiver can be tuned to any station in the band 4.0 MHz to 15 MHz. The corresponding wavelength band runs from $\\lambda_{min}$ to $\\lambda_{max}$. What is $\\lambda_{max} - \\lambda_{min}$? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s.)","image":"","options":["55 m","75 m","27.3 m","95 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\nu$ ranges from $4.0 \\times 10^{6}$ Hz to $15 \\times 10^{6}$ Hz.\nFormula: $\\lambda = \\frac{c}{\\nu}$. The lowest frequency gives the longest wavelength.\nSubstituting: $\\lambda_{max} = \\frac{3 \\times 10^8}{4 \\times 10^6} = 75$ m and $\\lambda_{min} = \\frac{3 \\times 10^8}{15 \\times 10^6} = 20$ m.\nSo the difference is 75 − 20 = 55 m. The 27.3 m option wrongly divides c by the frequency difference of 11 MHz.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":2,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A capacitor has two circular plates, each of radius 10 cm, separated by 2.0 mm of air. It is charged by a constant current of 0.20 A. What is the rate at which the potential difference between the plates increases? (Take $\\varepsilon_0 = 8.85 \\times 10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["$1.44 \\times 10^{9}$ V/s","$2.88 \\times 10^{9}$ V/s","$0.72 \\times 10^{9}$ V/s","$14.4 \\times 10^{9}$ V/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: r = 0.10 m, d = 2.0 × $10^{-3}$ m, i = 0.20 A.\nFormula: $C = \\frac{\\varepsilon_0 \\pi r^2}{d}$. Since Q = CV, we get i = C $\\frac{dV}{dt}$, so $\\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{i}{C}$.\nSubstituting: $C = \\frac{8.85 \\times 10^{-12} \\times 0.0314}{0.002} \\approx 1.39 \\times 10^{-10}$ F.\nThen $\\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{0.20}{1.39 \\times 10^{-10}} \\approx 1.44 \\times 10^{9}$ V/s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":3,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A monochromatic electromagnetic wave passes from vacuum into a non-magnetic medium ($\\mu_r = 1$) of relative permittivity 4.\nStatement I: In the medium, the frequency of the wave is unchanged and its wavelength becomes half of its value in vacuum.\nStatement II: In the medium, the ratio $\\frac{B_0}{E_0}$ remains equal to $\\frac{1}{c}$, as in vacuum.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The frequency is fixed by the source. The speed becomes $v = \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{4}} = \\frac{c}{2}$, so $\\lambda = \\frac{v}{\\nu}$ is halved.\nStatement II is incorrect. In vacuum, $\\frac{B_0}{E_0} = \\frac{1}{c}$ because the wave speed there is c. In the medium, c is replaced by v, so $\\frac{B_0}{E_0} = \\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{2}{c}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":4,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The magnetic field of a plane electromagnetic wave in vacuum is $\\vec{B} = 3 \\times 10^{-8}\\sin(kx + \\omega t)\\,\\hat{k}$ T. Which expression correctly gives its electric field (in V/m)? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s.)","image":"","options":["$\\vec{E} = 9\\sin(kx + \\omega t)\\,\\hat{j}$","$\\vec{E} = -9\\sin(kx + \\omega t)\\,\\hat{j}$","$\\vec{E} = 9\\sin(kx + \\omega t)\\,\\hat{i}$","$\\vec{E} = -9\\sin(kx - \\omega t)\\,\\hat{j}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Amplitude: $E_0 = B_0 c = 3 \\times 10^{-8} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8} = 9$ V/m.\nPhase: the phase $(kx + \\omega t)$ means the wave travels along −x, and E must share this same phase. This rules out option (4).\nAxis: E must be perpendicular to x and to z, so it lies along y. Option (3) puts E along the direction of travel and fails.\nSign: we need $\\vec{E} \\times \\vec{B}$ along $-\\hat{i}$. Since $\\hat{j} \\times \\hat{k} = +\\hat{i}$, E must be along $-\\hat{j}$. This rules out option (1).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":5,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about a plane electromagnetic wave travelling in vacuum:\n(i) The electric and magnetic fields oscillate in phase with each other.\n(ii) The amplitudes are related by $B_0 = E_0 c$.\n(iii) Its speed in vacuum does not depend on its wavelength.\n(iv) The vector $\\vec{E}\\times\\vec{B}$ points along the direction of propagation.\n(v) A material medium is needed for the electric and magnetic fields to oscillate.\nHow many of these statements are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $E_x$ and $B_y$ both vary as $\\sin(kz-\\omega t)$, so they are in phase.\n(ii) Incorrect: the correct relation is $B_0 = E_0/c$.\n(iii) Correct: experiments show the speed $c$ is the same for all wavelengths.\n(iv) Correct: $\\vec{E}\\times\\vec{B}$ points along the direction of propagation.\n(v) Incorrect: EM waves are self-sustaining and need no medium.\nThat makes 3 correct statements.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":6,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Inside a transparent medium, the electric field of a light wave is $E_x = 40\\sin(\\pi \\times 10^{7}\\,z - 2\\pi \\times 10^{15}\\,t)$ V/m (SI units). The wave then emerges into vacuum. What is its wavelength in vacuum? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s.)","image":"","options":["300 nm","200 nm","133 nm","450 nm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $k = \\pi \\times 10^{7}$ rad/m and $\\omega = 2\\pi \\times 10^{15}$ rad/s inside the medium.\nThe frequency is set by the source and stays the same in every medium: $\\nu = \\frac{\\omega}{2\\pi} = 10^{15}$ Hz.\nThe wavelength in the medium is $\\frac{2\\pi}{k} = 200$ nm. This is the tempting wrong option.\nIn vacuum, $\\lambda_0 = \\frac{c}{\\nu} = \\frac{3 \\times 10^8}{10^{15}} = 3 \\times 10^{-7}$ m.\nSo $\\lambda_0$ = 300 nm. This is consistent with the speed in the medium, $\\frac{\\omega}{k} = 2 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s, which gives n = 1.5.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":7,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A parallel plate capacitor has plates of area 0.010 m$^2$ in vacuum. At an instant, the current in the connecting wires is 0.50 A. Find the rate of change of the uniform electric field between the plates at that instant. (Take $\\varepsilon_0 = 8.85 \\times 10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["$5.65 \\times 10^{12}$ V m$^{-1}$ s$^{-1}$","$5.65 \\times 10^{10}$ V m$^{-1}$ s$^{-1}$","$1.13 \\times 10^{13}$ V m$^{-1}$ s$^{-1}$","$2.82 \\times 10^{12}$ V m$^{-1}$ s$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A = 0.010$ m$^2$ and $i = 0.50$ A. Between the plates, $i_d = i$.\nFormula: $i_d = \\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = \\varepsilon_0 A \\frac{dE}{dt}$, so $\\frac{dE}{dt} = \\frac{i}{\\varepsilon_0 A}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{dE}{dt} = \\frac{0.50}{8.85\\times10^{-12} \\times 0.010}$\nSo $\\frac{dE}{dt} \\approx 5.65\\times10^{12}$ V m$^{-1}$ s$^{-1}$\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":8,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"In Maxwell's analysis of a charging capacitor, a tiffin-box shaped surface has its flat circular bottom S between the plates. No current passes through S. Which physical quantity does pass through S and hints at the missing term in Ampere's law?","image":"","options":["Magnetic flux due to the wire","Electric field (electric flux) between the plates","Conduction electrons leaking across the gap","Magnetic monopoles"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Between the plates there is a uniform electric field perpendicular to S. Its magnitude is $\\frac{Q}{\\varepsilon_0 A}$.\nThis field gives an electric flux through S. When Q changes, this flux changes.\nMaxwell identified $\\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$ as the missing current term.\nNo charges cross the gap, so option (3) is wrong. Magnetic monopoles are not known to exist, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":9,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following lists electromagnetic radiations correctly in the order of increasing wavelength?","image":"","options":["γ-rays, X-rays, ultraviolet, visible, infrared, microwaves, radio waves","X-rays, γ-rays, ultraviolet, visible, microwaves, infrared, radio waves","Radio waves, microwaves, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-rays, γ-rays","γ-rays, ultraviolet, X-rays, visible, infrared, radio waves, microwaves"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The EM spectrum runs from γ-rays (about $10^{-12}$ m) to long radio waves (about $10^{6}$ m).\nIn order of increasing wavelength it is: γ-rays, X-rays, UV, visible, infrared, microwaves, radio waves.\nOption (3) gives the reverse order, which is decreasing wavelength.\nOptions (2) and (4) each swap two neighbouring regions.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":10,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A person sitting all day behind closed glass windows in bright sunshine does not get a sun tan. Which property explains this?","image":"","options":["Ordinary glass absorbs ultraviolet radiation","Ordinary glass absorbs visible light strongly","Ordinary glass converts infrared into ultraviolet radiation","Ordinary glass reflects all the radiation coming from the sun"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Tanning happens because UV exposure makes the skin produce more melanin.\nNCERT states that ordinary glass absorbs UV radiation, so no UV reaches the skin through a window.\nVisible light passes through glass easily, which is why the room is bright. So option (2) fails.\nGlass does not convert infrared into UV. It also does not reflect all sunlight.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":11,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A plane electromagnetic wave travels in vacuum. The amplitude of its electric field is 90 V/m. What is the amplitude of its magnetic field? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s)","image":"","options":["$3 \\times 10^{-7}$ T","$3 \\times 10^{-6}$ T","$2.7 \\times 10^{10}$ T","$3 \\times 10^{-8}$ T"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $E_0 = 90$ V/m and $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s.\nFormula: $B_0 = \\frac{E_0}{c}$\nSubstituting: $B_0 = \\frac{90}{3 \\times 10^{8}}$\nSo $B_0 = 3 \\times 10^{-7}$ T.\nThe option $2.7 \\times 10^{10}$ T comes from wrongly multiplying $E_0$ by c.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":12,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A linearly polarised plane electromagnetic wave travels along the +z direction. At a given instant, its electric field oscillates along the x-axis and varies sinusoidally with z. Along which axis does the magnetic field of this wave oscillate?","image":"","options":["Along the z-axis","Along the y-axis","Along the x-axis, in phase with E","It has no fixed direction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In an EM wave, E and B are perpendicular to each other and to the direction of propagation.\nPropagation is along z and E is along x, so B must lie along the remaining axis, y.\nIt cannot be along z, because the wave is transverse. It cannot be along x, because B must be perpendicular to E.\nThis is the wave described by $E_x = E_0 \\sin(kz - \\omega t)$ and $B_y = B_0 \\sin(kz - \\omega t)$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":13,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a plane electromagnetic wave in vacuum, the electric and magnetic fields at any point reach their maximum values at the same instant.\nReason (R): The amplitudes of the two fields are related by $B_0 = \\frac{E_0}{c}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: $E_x = E_0 \\sin(kz - \\omega t)$ and $B_y = B_0 \\sin(kz - \\omega t)$ have the same phase. So E and B peak together and become zero together.\nR is true: Maxwell's equations give $B_0 = \\frac{E_0}{c}$.\nHowever, R only relates the sizes of the amplitudes. It says nothing about phase, so it does not explain A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":14,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A charge moving with constant velocity does not radiate electromagnetic waves.\nReason (R): A charge in uniform motion produces no magnetic field.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Only accelerated charges radiate electromagnetic waves, and a charge moving with constant velocity is not accelerating.\nR is false. A charge in uniform motion is a steady current, and a steady current does produce a magnetic field. That field simply does not vary with time.\nA time-varying field is what is needed to regenerate the wave, so no wave is produced.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":15,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The division of the electromagnetic spectrum into regions such as radio waves, microwaves and infrared is based roughly on","image":"","options":["the speed of the waves in vacuum","how the waves are produced and/or detected","whether the waves are transverse or longitudinal","the sharply defined frequency limits fixed by Maxwell's equations"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the classification is based roughly on how the waves are produced and/or detected.\nThe regions overlap and have no sharp boundaries.\nAll EM waves travel at the same speed $c$ in vacuum, so speed cannot separate them.\nAll EM waves are transverse.\nMaxwell's equations do not fix any boundary between regions.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":16,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"An electromagnetic wave travelling in vacuum has a frequency of $5 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz. Find its wavelength. (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s.)","image":"","options":["60 nm","600 nm","1500 nm","6000 nm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\nu = 5 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz and $c = 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s.\nFormula: $\\lambda = \\frac{c}{\\nu}$.\nSubstituting: $\\lambda = \\frac{3 \\times 10^8}{5 \\times 10^{14}} = 6 \\times 10^{-7}$ m.\nSo $\\lambda$ = 600 nm. This lies inside the visible range of 400–700 nm.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":17,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following will radiate electromagnetic waves?\n(i) A charge at rest\n(ii) A charge moving with constant velocity in a straight line\n(iii) A charge executing simple harmonic oscillation\n(iv) A charge moving in a circle with uniform speed\n(v) A steady current in a long straight wire\n(vi) A fast electron being decelerated on striking a metal target","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Only accelerated charges radiate electromagnetic waves.\n(i) No: a charge at rest produces only an electrostatic field.\n(ii) and (v) No: uniform motion gives a magnetic field that does not change with time.\n(iii) Yes: an oscillating charge is accelerating.\n(iv) Yes: uniform circular motion involves centripetal acceleration.\n(vi) Yes: deceleration is acceleration; this is how X-rays are produced.\nSo 3 cases radiate.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":18,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The plates of a parallel plate capacitor each have area 0.02 m$^2$. While it is being charged, the uniform electric field between the plates increases at the rate $5.0 \\times 10^{12}$ V m$^{-1}$ s$^{-1}$. Find the displacement current between the plates. (Take $\\epsilon_0 = 8.85 \\times 10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$; neglect fringing.)","image":"","options":["8.85 A","0.0885 A","0.885 A","1.77 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $A = 0.02$ m$^2$ and $\\frac{dE}{dt} = 5.0\\times10^{12}$ V m$^{-1}$ s$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\Phi_E = EA$, so $i_d = \\epsilon_0 A\\frac{dE}{dt}$\nSubstituting: $i_d = 8.85\\times10^{-12}\\times0.02\\times5.0\\times10^{12}$\nSo $i_d$ = 0.885 A.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":19,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Green light of wavelength 500 nm is used in an experiment. What is the energy of one photon of this light in electron-volts? (Take $h = 6.63 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s, $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s, $1$ eV $= 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ J)","image":"","options":["2.49 eV","0.25 eV","24.9 eV","4.97 eV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\lambda = 500\\times10^{-9}$ m.\nFormula: $E = h\\nu = \\frac{hc}{\\lambda}$.\nSubstituting: $E = \\frac{6.63\\times10^{-34}\\times3\\times10^{8}}{5\\times10^{-7}} = 3.98\\times10^{-19}$ J.\nConverting to eV: $\\frac{3.98\\times10^{-19}}{1.6\\times10^{-19}} \\approx 2.49$ eV.\nA few eV is typical of visible light, which comes from electron transitions in atoms.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":20,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The FM radio band extends from 88 MHz to 108 MHz. What is the longest wavelength of electromagnetic waves in this band? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s)","image":"","options":["3.41 m","2.78 m","0.341 m","34.1 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the band runs from 88 MHz to 108 MHz, and $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s.\nFormula: $\\lambda = \\frac{c}{\\nu}$. The longest wavelength comes from the lowest frequency, 88 MHz.\nSubstituting: $\\lambda = \\frac{3 \\times 10^{8}}{88 \\times 10^{6}}$\nSo $\\lambda \\approx 3.41$ m.\nThe value 2.78 m is the shortest wavelength, obtained from 108 MHz.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":21,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A parallel plate capacitor is being charged by a time-dependent current $i(t)$.\nStatement I: In the region between the plates, the conduction current is zero.\nStatement II: In the region between the plates, the displacement current equals the current $i(t)$ in the connecting wires.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. No charges flow across the gap between the plates, so $i_c = 0$ there.\nStatement II: Correct. Since $\\Phi_E = Q/\\epsilon_0$, we get $\\epsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = \\frac{dQ}{dt} = i$. So $i_d = i$ between the plates.\nOutside the plates the situation is reversed: $i_c = i$ and $i_d = 0$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":22,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Consider a charging parallel plate capacitor and a circular loop around its connecting wire. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) By symmetry, B is tangential to the loop and has the same magnitude at all points on it.\n(ii) The missing term in Ampere's law must give the same B at point P whatever surface is chosen.\n(iii) The missing term equals $\\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$ and has the dimensions of current.\n(iv) With the missing term added, B at a point just inside the plates becomes zero.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. Symmetry about the wire makes B circumferential and uniform in magnitude on the loop.\n(ii) Correct. Removing the dependence on the chosen surface is exactly why the term is needed.\n(iii) Correct. Since $\\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = \\frac{dQ}{dt}$, the term is a current.\n(iv) Incorrect. B at a point M just inside the plates equals B at P just outside; it is not zero.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":23,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is the source of gamma rays?","image":"","options":["Bombardment of a metal target by high-energy electrons","Klystron and magnetron valves","Electrons in atoms moving from an outer energy level to a lower one","Nuclear reactions and radioactive decay of nuclei"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Gamma rays are the highest-frequency electromagnetic waves.\nThey are produced in nuclear reactions and are emitted by radioactive nuclei (Table 8.1: radioactive decay of the nucleus).\nOption (1) describes how X-rays are produced in an X-ray tube.\nOption (2) describes sources of microwaves.\nOption (3) describes how visible light is emitted by atoms.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":24,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The magnetic field of a plane electromagnetic wave in vacuum is $\\vec{B} = 4 \\times 10^{-8}\\sin(2.0 \\times 10^{2}\\,y - 6.0 \\times 10^{10}\\,t)\\,\\hat{i}$ T (SI units). Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Wavelength\n(b) Frequency\n(c) Amplitude of electric field\n(d) Axis along which E oscillates\nList-II\n(i) 12 V/m\n(ii) 3.14 cm\n(iii) 9.55 GHz\n(iv) z-axis","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $\\lambda = \\frac{2\\pi}{k} = \\frac{2\\pi}{200} = 0.0314$ m, which is 3.14 cm → (ii).\n(b) $\\nu = \\frac{\\omega}{2\\pi} = \\frac{6 \\times 10^{10}}{2\\pi} \\approx 9.55 \\times 10^{9}$ Hz → (iii).\n(c) $E_0 = B_0 c = 4 \\times 10^{-8} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8} = 12$ V/m → (i).\n(d) The wave travels along y and B is along x. E must be perpendicular to both, so it lies along z ($\\hat{k} \\times \\hat{i} = \\hat{j}$) → (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":25,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about infrared waves are correct?\n(i) The infrared band lies next to the low-frequency (long-wavelength) end of the visible spectrum.\n(ii) Infrared waves are called heat waves because molecules such as water readily absorb them and their thermal motion increases.\n(iii) Infrared waves set only electrons into vibration and cannot vibrate whole atoms or molecules.\n(iv) Semiconductor light emitting diodes emitting infrared are used in remote switches of TV sets.\n(v) Infrared detectors in earth satellites are used to observe the growth of crops.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: infrared is adjacent to the long-wavelength end of the visible spectrum.\n(ii) Correct: water absorbs infrared readily and heats up, hence the name heat waves.\n(iii) Incorrect: infrared vibrates entire atoms or molecules, not just electrons, and this raises the internal energy.\n(iv) Correct: infrared LEDs are used in TV remote controls.\n(v) Correct: satellite infrared detectors are used for military purposes and to observe crop growth.\nSo four statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":26,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0}}$\n(B) $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu\\varepsilon}}$\n(C) $\\frac{E_0}{c}$\n(D) $\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu\\varepsilon}{\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0}}$\nList-II\n(i) Speed of em waves in a material medium\n(ii) Magnetic field amplitude of an em wave in vacuum\n(iii) Speed of light in vacuum\n(iv) Refractive index of the medium with respect to vacuum","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0}} = c$, the speed of light in vacuum, so A-(iii).\n(B) $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu\\varepsilon}} = v$, the speed in a medium, so B-(i).\n(C) $\\frac{E_0}{c} = B_0$, the magnetic field amplitude, so C-(ii).\n(D) $\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu\\varepsilon}{\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0}} = \\frac{c}{v}$, the ratio of speeds, which is the refractive index, so D-(iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":27,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"An electromagnetic wave in vacuum is described by $E_y = 50\\sin\\left[2\\pi\\left(\\frac{z}{0.6} - 5 \\times 10^{8}\\,t\\right)\\right]$ V/m, with z in metres and t in seconds. Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["Its wavelength is 0.6 m and its frequency is $5 \\times 10^{8}$ Hz","Its wave number k is 0.6 rad/m","It propagates along the −z direction","Its magnetic field oscillates along the y-axis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Compare the expression with $E_0\\sin\\left[2\\pi\\left(\\frac{z}{\\lambda} - \\nu t\\right)\\right]$.\n(1) Correct: $\\lambda = 0.6$ m and $\\nu = 5 \\times 10^{8}$ Hz. Check: $\\nu\\lambda = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s = c.\n(2) Wrong: $k = \\frac{2\\pi}{0.6} \\approx 10.5$ rad/m.\n(3) Wrong: the phase has a minus sign, $(z/\\lambda - \\nu t)$, so the wave moves along +z.\n(4) Wrong: E is already along y, so B must be along x.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":28,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"In vacuum, the electric field of a plane electromagnetic wave is $E_x = E_0 \\sin(kz - \\omega t)$. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The magnetic field oscillates along the y-axis.\n(ii) The magnetic field amplitude is $B_0 = cE_0$.\n(iii) The wave propagates along the +z direction.\n(iv) The speed of the wave is $\\frac{\\omega}{k}$, which equals $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0 \\varepsilon_0}}$.\n(v) The electric and magnetic fields oscillate with a phase difference of $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: B must be perpendicular to both E (along x) and the propagation direction (along z), so it lies along y.\n(ii) Incorrect: the correct relation is $B_0 = \\frac{E_0}{c}$.\n(iii) Correct: a phase of the form $kz - \\omega t$ describes travel along +z.\n(iv) Correct: $\\omega = ck$ and $c = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0}}$.\n(v) Incorrect: $E_x$ and $B_y$ both vary as $\\sin(kz - \\omega t)$, so they are in phase.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":29,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A monochromatic beam of visible light has a frequency of $4.5 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz in vacuum. Take $c = 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s. What is its wavelength?","image":"","options":["667 nm","450 nm","1500 nm","333 nm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\nu = 4.5 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz, $c = 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s\nFormula: $\\lambda = \\frac{c}{\\nu}$\nSubstituting: $\\lambda = \\frac{3 \\times 10^8}{4.5 \\times 10^{14}} = 6.67 \\times 10^{-7}$ m\nSo $\\lambda \\approx 667$ nm, which lies near the red end of the 400–700 nm visible range.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":30,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"In a microwave oven, food containing water gets heated efficiently because the frequency of the microwaves is chosen to","image":"","options":["match the resonant frequency of water molecules","be equal to the frequency of visible light","ionise the water molecules in the food","match the frequency of infrared radiation emitted by the food"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT: the oven frequency is selected to match the resonant frequency of water molecules.\nAt resonance, energy from the waves passes efficiently into the kinetic energy of the molecules, so the temperature of the food rises.\nMicrowaves are far below visible frequencies, so (2) is wrong.\nThey do not ionise molecules, so (3) is wrong.\nThe heating is not due to matching infrared, so (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":31,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Maxwell's displacement current through a surface is given by which expression? ($\\Phi_E$ is the electric flux through that surface.)","image":"","options":["$\\mu_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$","$\\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$","$\\frac{1}{\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$","$\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0 \\Phi_E$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Between the plates, $\\Phi_E = Q/\\varepsilon_0$.\nDifferentiating gives $\\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = \\frac{1}{\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{dQ}{dt}$. So $\\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = i$, which is the displacement current $i_d$.\nThe term $\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$ appears on the right side of the Ampere-Maxwell law. That is $\\mu_0 i_d$, not $i_d$ itself.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":32,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"For a plane electromagnetic wave in vacuum described by $E_x = E_0 \\sin(kz - \\omega t)$ and $B_y = B_0 \\sin(kz - \\omega t)$, consider the following statements:\n(i) The electric and magnetic fields oscillate in phase.\n(ii) The wave propagates along the +z direction.\n(iii) $B_0 = cE_0$.\n(iv) The speed of the wave is $\\frac{\\omega}{k}$.\n(v) The wave vector $\\vec{k}$ points along the x-axis.\nHow many of these statements are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: both fields have the same phase $(kz-\\omega t)$.\n(ii) Correct: the form $(kz-\\omega t)$ represents a wave moving along +z.\n(iii) Incorrect: the correct relation is $B_0 = \\frac{E_0}{c}$.\n(iv) Correct: the wave speed is $\\frac{\\omega}{k} = c$.\n(v) Incorrect: $\\vec{k}$ points along the propagation direction, z, not along $\\vec{E}$.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":33,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A capacitor with circular plates of radius R is being charged by a current i. Assume the displacement current is uniform over the plate area and edge effects are negligible. Point M lies between the plates at distance R/2 from the axis. Point P lies outside the plates, near the connecting wire (taken as a long straight wire along the axis), at distance 2R from the axis. Find the ratio $B_M : B_P$ of the magnetic field magnitudes.","image":"","options":["1 : 1","1 : 2","2 : 1","1 : 4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"At M: the loop of radius R/2 encloses $i_d \\frac{(R/2)^2}{R^2} = \\frac{i}{4}$.\nSo $B_M (2\\pi \\frac{R}{2}) = \\frac{\\mu_0 i}{4}$, giving $B_M = \\frac{\\mu_0 i}{4\\pi R}$.\nAt P: only conduction current i links the loop, so $B_P (2\\pi \\cdot 2R) = \\mu_0 i$, giving $B_P = \\frac{\\mu_0 i}{4\\pi R}$.\nRatio $B_M : B_P = 1 : 1$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":34,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The first experimental demonstration of electromagnetic waves was made in the radio-wave region and not with visible light.\nReason (R): The frequency of visible light (about $6 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz for yellow light) is far higher than the frequency of about $10^{11}$ Hz that can be obtained even with modern electronic circuits.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Hertz's 1887 demonstration used low-frequency radio waves.\nR is true. Yellow light has a frequency of about $6 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz, while circuits reach hardly about $10^{11}$ Hz.\nNo ac circuit can make charges oscillate at optical frequencies, so the test had to be done at radio frequencies. R therefore explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":35,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Carbon dioxide and water vapour in the atmosphere help maintain the average temperature of the earth.\nReason (R): Visible sunlight absorbed by the earth's surface is re-radiated as longer-wavelength infrared radiation, which is trapped by these greenhouse gases.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that infrared radiation maintains the earth's warmth through the greenhouse effect.\nR is true. Incoming visible light passes easily through the atmosphere and is absorbed by the surface.\nThe surface re-radiates this energy as infrared, which CO$_2$ and water vapour trap.\nThis trapping of infrared is exactly why the earth stays warm, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":36,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A flat disc surface and a pot-shaped surface share the same circular rim around the wire of a charging capacitor. When the original Ampere's law is applied to both, which side of $\\oint \\vec{B}\\cdot d\\vec{l} = \\mu_0 i$ remains the same for the two surfaces?","image":"","options":["Only the right-hand side","Only the left-hand side","Both sides","Neither side"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The line integral $\\oint \\vec{B}\\cdot d\\vec{l}$ depends only on the closed loop, which is the common rim. So the left-hand side equals $B(2\\pi r)$ for both surfaces.\nThe right-hand side depends on the current crossing the chosen surface. It is $\\mu_0 i$ for the flat disc and zero for the pot.\nThis mismatch is the contradiction Maxwell noticed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":37,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of radiation) with List-II (method of production):\nList-I\n(A) Radio waves\n(B) Microwaves\n(C) Infrared\n(D) Gamma rays\nList-II\n(I) Radioactive decay of the nucleus\n(II) Vibration of atoms and molecules\n(III) Klystron or magnetron valve\n(IV) Rapid acceleration and deceleration of electrons in aerials","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) Radio waves are produced by accelerating electrons in aerials, so A-IV.\n(B) Microwaves come from klystron or magnetron valves, so B-III.\n(C) Infrared is produced by vibrating atoms and molecules (hot bodies), so C-II.\n(D) Gamma rays are emitted in radioactive decay of nuclei, so D-I.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":38,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The speed of electromagnetic waves in vacuum is used to define a standard of length. Which experimental fact makes this possible?","image":"","options":["The speed is the same for all wavelengths, within a few metres per second, and its value is very accurately known","The speed is greater for shorter wavelengths, so gamma rays give the most precise standard","The speed depends on the permittivity and permeability of the medium through which the wave travels","The speed equals the frequency of the source multiplied by its amplitude"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that experiments with em waves of different wavelengths give the same speed in vacuum, within a few metres per second of $3 \\times 10^8$ m/s.\nThis constancy, together with the very precisely known value, is why the speed of light is used to define a standard of length.\nOption (2) is wrong because the speed in vacuum does not depend on wavelength.\nOption (3) is true for a material medium, but it is not the reason the vacuum speed serves as a standard.\nOption (4) is not a valid relation: $v = \\nu\\lambda$, and amplitude does not enter.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":39,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The electric field of a plane electromagnetic wave in vacuum is $E_x = 50 \\sin[(2\\pi \\times 10^{4})z - \\omega t]$ V/m, where z is in metres. To which region of the electromagnetic spectrum does this wave belong, and what is $\\omega$? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s)","image":"","options":["Infrared; $\\omega = 6\\pi \\times 10^{12}$ rad/s","Microwave; $\\omega = 6\\pi \\times 10^{12}$ rad/s","Infrared; $\\omega = 3 \\times 10^{12}$ rad/s","Ultraviolet; $\\omega = 6\\pi \\times 10^{12}$ rad/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"From the equation, $k = 2\\pi \\times 10^{4}$ rad/m, so $\\lambda = 2\\pi/k = 10^{-4}$ m = 0.1 mm.\nPer NCERT Table 8.1, infrared spans 1 mm to 700 nm. So 0.1 mm is infrared, not microwave (0.1 m to 1 mm).\nUsing $\\omega = ck$: $\\omega = 3 \\times 10^{8} \\times 2\\pi \\times 10^{4} = 6\\pi \\times 10^{12}$ rad/s.\nThe value $3 \\times 10^{12}$ is the frequency $\\nu$ in Hz, not $\\omega$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":40,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the speed of electromagnetic waves in vacuum is correct?","image":"","options":["Gamma rays travel faster than radio waves because they have a higher frequency.","The speed is the same for all wavelengths, and its known value is used to define a standard of length.","The speed depends on the amplitude of the electric field of the wave.","Radio waves travel faster than visible light because they have a longer wavelength."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Experiments show that the speed of em waves in vacuum is the same for all wavelengths, to within a few m/s of $3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s.\nThis speed is so well established that it is used to define the standard of length.\nThe speed does not depend on frequency, wavelength or amplitude, so options (1), (3) and (4) are wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":41,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The amplitude of the electric field in a plane electromagnetic wave travelling in vacuum is 30 V/m. What is the amplitude of the magnetic field? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s)","image":"","options":["$1 \\times 10^{-7}$ T","$9 \\times 10^{9}$ T","$1 \\times 10^{-8}$ T","$3 \\times 10^{-7}$ T"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $E_0 = 30$ V/m, $c = 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s.\nFormula: $B_0 = \\frac{E_0}{c}$.\nSubstituting: $B_0 = \\frac{30}{3 \\times 10^8}$.\nSo $B_0 = 1 \\times 10^{-7}$ T. The option $9 \\times 10^9$ T comes from multiplying by c instead of dividing.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":42,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Some animals can sense radiation that lies outside the range visible to humans. Which pairing is correct according to NCERT?","image":"","options":["Snakes – infrared; many insects – ultraviolet","Snakes – ultraviolet; many insects – infrared","Snakes – X-rays; many insects – microwaves","Snakes – microwaves; many insects – infrared"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that different animals are sensitive to different ranges of wavelengths.\nSnakes can detect infrared waves.\nThe 'visible' range of many insects extends well into the ultraviolet.\nOption (2) swaps the two animals, so it is the most tempting wrong choice.\nNo animal is described as sensing X-rays or microwaves.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":43,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The magnetic field in a plane electromagnetic wave travelling in vacuum has an amplitude of $4 \\times 10^{-8}$ T. What is the amplitude of the electric field? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s)","image":"","options":["12 V/m","120 V/m","1.2 V/m","0.012 V/m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $B_0 = 4 \\times 10^{-8}$ T and $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s.\nFormula: $E_0 = B_0 c$.\nSubstituting: $E_0 = 4 \\times 10^{-8} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}$.\nSo $E_0$ = 12 V/m.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":44,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a wave in vacuum with $\\vec{E} = E_0\\sin(kz - \\omega t)\\,\\hat{i}$, the accompanying magnetic field is $\\vec{B} = \\frac{E_0}{c}\\sin(kz - \\omega t)\\,\\hat{j}$.\nReason (R): In an electromagnetic wave, E and B oscillate in phase, $B_0 = \\frac{E_0}{c}$, and $\\vec{E} \\times \\vec{B}$ points along the direction of propagation.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: E and B have the same phase $(kz - \\omega t)$, and NCERT gives $B_0 = \\frac{E_0}{c}$. Also, $\\vec{E} \\times \\vec{B}$ gives the direction of propagation.\nA is true: the wave travels along +z, and $\\hat{i} \\times \\hat{j} = \\hat{k}$. So B along $+\\hat{j}$ with amplitude $\\frac{E_0}{c}$ and the same phase is correct.\nA follows directly from the three facts stated in R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":45,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Light from the sun reaches the earth even though most of the space in between is nearly a vacuum.\nReason (R): Electromagnetic waves are self-sustaining oscillations of electric and magnetic fields, and no material medium is involved in these vibrations.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: sunlight crosses nearly empty space to reach the earth.\nR is true: NCERT describes EM waves as self-sustaining oscillations of $\\vec{E}$ and $\\vec{B}$. A changing E regenerates B and vice versa, without any material medium.\nR explains A, because no medium is needed for light to propagate through vacuum.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":46,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A parallel plate capacitor is being charged by a current i.\nStatement I: In the connecting wires outside the plates, the conduction current is i and the displacement current is zero.\nStatement II: In the region between the plates, the conduction current is zero and the displacement current $\\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$ equals i.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Outside the plates, charges flow in the wire, so $i_c = i$. The field in the wire is not changing in the way needed to give a displacement current, so $i_d = 0$.\nStatement II is correct. No charge crosses the gap, so $i_c = 0$. The changing electric flux there gives $i_d = \\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = \\frac{dQ}{dt} = i$.\nThe total current $i_c + i_d$ is therefore the same everywhere in the circuit.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":47,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A parallel plate capacitor is being charged by a time-varying current. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In the region between the plates, the conduction current is zero and the displacement current equals the current in the connecting wires.\n(ii) A displacement current exists through a surface only if the electric flux through it changes with time.\n(iii) A straight wire carrying a steady current has a non-zero displacement current inside it.\n(iv) Displacement current produces a magnetic field in exactly the same way as conduction current.\n(v) The magnetic field at a point just inside the gap, near the edge of the plates, is zero because no charges flow there.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: between the plates, $i_c = 0$ and $i_d = i$.\n(ii) Correct: $i_d = \\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$, which is zero if the flux is constant.\n(iii) Incorrect: with a steady current, E does not change with time, so $i_d = 0$.\n(iv) Correct: displacement current acts as a source of B just like conduction current.\n(v) Incorrect: B just inside (at M) is the same as just outside (at P).\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":48,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The AM radio band extends from 530 kHz to 1710 kHz. What is the longest wavelength used in this band? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s)","image":"","options":["about 566 m","about 175 m","about 5.66 m","about 1760 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the AM band is 530–1710 kHz and $c = 3\\times10^{8}$ m/s.\nFormula: $\\lambda = \\frac{c}{\\nu}$. The longest wavelength corresponds to the lowest frequency, 530 kHz.\nSubstituting: $\\lambda = \\frac{3\\times10^{8}}{530\\times10^{3}} \\approx 566$ m.\nThe value 175 m comes from 1710 kHz, which gives the shortest wavelength instead.\nSo the longest wavelength is about 566 m.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":49,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following will NOT radiate electromagnetic waves?","image":"","options":["Electrons oscillating up and down in a transmitting antenna","An electron moving in a circle with constant speed","Electrons drifting at constant velocity in a wire carrying a steady current","Fast electrons being suddenly stopped by a metal target"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Only accelerated charges radiate electromagnetic waves.\n(1) Oscillating charges are accelerated, so they radiate.\n(2) Uniform circular motion still involves centripetal acceleration, so the charge radiates. This is the tempting option.\n(3) A steady current means charges in uniform motion. This gives a magnetic field that does not change with time, so there is no radiation.\n(4) Sudden deceleration is acceleration, and this is how X-rays are produced.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":50,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A capacitor with circular plates of radius R is being charged by a constant current i. A circular loop of radius $r = \\frac{R}{2}$ is placed between the plates, coaxial with them and parallel to them. Neglect edge effects. Which option correctly gives the displacement current through the loop and the magnitude of the magnetic field on the loop?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{i}{4}$ and $\\frac{\\mu_0 i}{4\\pi R}$","$\\frac{i}{2}$ and $\\frac{\\mu_0 i}{2\\pi R}$","$i$ and $\\frac{\\mu_0 i}{\\pi R}$","zero and zero, since no conduction current passes through the loop"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The field E between the plates is uniform, so the flux through the loop is a fraction $\\frac{\\pi r^2}{\\pi R^2} = \\frac{1}{4}$ of the total flux.\nTherefore $i_d = \\frac{i}{4}$ through the loop.\nApplying the Ampere-Maxwell law: $B(2\\pi r) = \\mu_0 \\frac{i}{4}$, so $B = \\frac{\\mu_0 i}{8\\pi r} = \\frac{\\mu_0 i}{4\\pi R}$.\nOption (3) wrongly takes the whole current as enclosed. Option (4) ignores the displacement current, which also produces a magnetic field.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":51,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Light of frequency $5 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz enters a non-magnetic transparent medium ($\\mu_r = 1$) of relative permittivity 2.25. What is its wavelength inside the medium? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s)","image":"","options":["400 nm","600 nm","267 nm","900 nm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\nu = 5 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz, $\\varepsilon_r = 2.25$, $\\mu_r = 1$.\nFormula: $v = \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_r \\mu_r}}$ and $\\lambda = \\frac{v}{\\nu}$. The frequency does not change when light enters the medium.\nSubstituting: $v = \\frac{3 \\times 10^{8}}{1.5} = 2 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s, so $\\lambda = \\frac{2 \\times 10^{8}}{5 \\times 10^{14}} = 4 \\times 10^{-7}$ m.\nSo $\\lambda = 400$ nm.\nOption (2), 600 nm, is the wavelength in vacuum.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":52,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Match the wavelength in List-I with the region of the electromagnetic spectrum in List-II:\nList-I\n(A) 10 $\\mu$m\n(B) 2 cm\n(C) 50 nm\n(D) 0.05 nm\nList-II\n(I) X-rays\n(II) Infrared\n(III) Ultraviolet\n(IV) Microwaves","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) 10 $\\mu$m = $10^{-5}$ m lies in the infrared range (1 mm to 700 nm), so A-II.\n(B) 2 cm lies in the microwave range (0.1 m to 1 mm), so B-IV.\n(C) 50 nm lies in the ultraviolet range (400 nm to 1 nm), so C-III.\n(D) 0.05 nm lies in the X-ray range (1 nm to $10^{-3}$ nm), so D-I.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":53,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Even after Maxwell added the displacement current, the laws of electricity and magnetism are not perfectly symmetrical.\nReason (R): There are no known magnetic monopoles that could act as sources of magnetic field the way electric charges act as sources of electric field.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The displacement current adds symmetry: a changing E produces B, just as a changing B produces E (Faraday's law).\nA is true. NCERT notes that the laws are still not perfectly symmetrical.\nR is true. Gauss's law for magnetism has zero on the right side because no magnetic charge is known.\nR explains A. The asymmetry exists precisely because electric charge has no magnetic counterpart.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":54,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A radio station broadcasts at a carrier frequency of 1000 kHz. According to NCERT's classification of radio bands, this transmission falls in which band?","image":"","options":["FM radio band","AM radio band","TV band","Microwave band"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The AM band runs from 530 kHz to 1710 kHz.\nThe FM band runs from 88 MHz to 108 MHz.\nThe TV band runs from 54 MHz to 890 MHz.\n1000 kHz equals 1 MHz. This lies within 530–1710 kHz, so the station is in the AM band.\nThe FM band is the tempting wrong choice, but 1 MHz is far below 88 MHz.\nMicrowaves are in the GHz range, so they are also ruled out.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":55,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A capacitor with circular plates of radius 5 cm is charged by a steady current of 2 A. A circular loop of radius 3 cm lies between the plates, parallel to them and coaxial with them. Treat the electric field as uniform over the plate area and zero outside it. Find the magnitude of the magnetic field at points on the loop. ($\\mu_0 = 4\\pi\\times10^{-7}$ T m/A)","image":"","options":["4.8 μT","13.3 μT","8.0 μT","2.9 μT"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: R = 0.05 m, r = 0.03 m, i = 2 A.\nFormula: the displacement current enclosed by the loop is $i_d = i\\frac{r^2}{R^2}$, because the field is uniform over the plates. Ampere-Maxwell law gives $B(2\\pi r) = \\mu_0 i\\frac{r^2}{R^2}$, so $B = \\frac{\\mu_0 i r}{2\\pi R^2}$.\nSubstituting: $B = \\frac{2\\times10^{-7}\\times2\\times0.03}{0.0025} = 4.8\\times10^{-6}$ T.\nSo B = 4.8 μT.\nOption (2) wrongly uses the full current 2 A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":56,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Why could the electromagnetic nature of light not be tested simply by building an ac circuit whose current oscillates at the frequency of yellow light?","image":"","options":["Yellow light has a frequency of about $6 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz, while electronic circuits can reach only about $10^{11}$ Hz","An oscillating current produces only a magnetic field and no electric field","Visible light needs a material medium, but circuits radiate only in vacuum","Oscillating charges radiate waves whose frequency is half the frequency of oscillation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: yellow light has a frequency of about $6 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz. Even modern electronic circuits reach only about $10^{11}$ Hz.\nFor this reason, Hertz had to demonstrate electromagnetic waves in the low-frequency (radio) region.\nThe last option is tempting but wrong: the frequency of the emitted wave equals the frequency of the oscillating charge, not half of it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":57,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Using the wavelength ranges of Table 8.1 (microwave: 0.1 m to 1 mm), how many of the following frequencies fall in the microwave region? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s)\n100 MHz, 1 GHz, 5 GHz, 25 GHz, 500 GHz","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: the microwave region is $\\lambda$ from 0.1 m to 1 mm.\nFormula: $\\lambda = \\frac{c}{\\nu}$. This range corresponds to 3 GHz to 300 GHz.\n100 MHz gives 3 m, which is radio. 1 GHz gives 0.3 m, which is also radio.\n5 GHz gives 6 cm and 25 GHz gives 1.2 cm, so both are microwaves.\n500 GHz gives 0.6 mm, which is infrared.\nSo two of the frequencies lie in the microwave region.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":58,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"What was the most important prediction that came out of Maxwell's equations?","image":"","options":["Electric charge is made up of discrete particles","Coupled time-varying electric and magnetic fields can travel through space as electromagnetic waves","Magnetic monopoles exist as sources of magnetic field","A steady current in a wire radiates energy continuously"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT says the most important prediction of Maxwell's equations is electromagnetic waves. These are coupled, time-varying E and B fields that travel through space.\nOption (1) is wrong. Maxwell actually disagreed with the idea that electricity is particulate.\nOption (3) is wrong because no magnetic monopoles are known.\nOption (4) is wrong because a steady current gives a time-independent magnetic field and does not radiate.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":59,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Hertz demonstrated electromagnetic waves in the radio region rather than with visible light produced by an ac circuit. What is the main reason?","image":"","options":["Visible light cannot travel through air","Electronic circuits can reach only about $10^{11}$ Hz, while yellow light has a frequency of about $6 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz","Radio waves travel faster than visible light in vacuum","Visible light is not an electromagnetic wave"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An oscillating current radiates waves at its own frequency. To produce yellow light, charges would have to oscillate at about $6 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz.\nEven modern electronic circuits reach only about $10^{11}$ Hz, so the test had to be done in the low-frequency radio region.\nOption (3) is wrong because all EM waves have the same speed c in vacuum.\nOption (4) is wrong because light is an EM wave.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":60,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Infrared waves are called heat waves because they set entire atoms and molecules vibrating, which raises the internal energy and temperature of the substance.\nStatement II: In the greenhouse effect, the earth's surface absorbs incoming visible light and re-radiates it as radiation of shorter wavelength, which greenhouse gases trap.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. Infrared vibrates whole atoms and molecules, not just electrons, so it heats the material.\nStatement II is false. The re-radiated radiation is infrared, which has a LONGER wavelength than visible light. CO$_2$ and water vapour trap this infrared.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":61,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about electromagnetic waves in free space is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["They are self-sustaining oscillations of electric and magnetic fields","Their electric and magnetic fields are perpendicular to the direction of propagation","They need a material medium whose particles vibrate to carry the wave","All of them travel with the same speed c, regardless of wavelength"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the changing E and B fields regenerate each other, so the wave sustains itself.\n(2) Correct: EM waves are transverse.\n(3) Incorrect: unlike sound or string waves, no material medium is involved. Only the fields oscillate, so EM waves travel through vacuum.\n(4) Correct: experiments show c is independent of wavelength, to within a few m/s.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":62,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II for electromagnetic waves ($\\mu_r$ and $\\varepsilon_r$ are the relative permeability and relative permittivity of the medium).\nList-I:\n(A) Speed of em waves in vacuum\n(B) Ratio $\\frac{B_0}{E_0}$ for a wave in vacuum\n(C) Speed of em waves in a medium\n(D) Refractive index of the medium with respect to vacuum\nList-II:\n(i) $\\frac{1}{c}$\n(ii) $\\sqrt{\\mu_r \\varepsilon_r}$\n(iii) $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0 \\varepsilon_0}}$\n(iv) $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu \\varepsilon}}$","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) In vacuum, $c = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0}}$, so A matches (iii).\n(B) $E_0 = cB_0$, so $\\frac{B_0}{E_0} = \\frac{1}{c}$, so B matches (i).\n(C) In a medium, $v = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu\\varepsilon}}$, so C matches (iv).\n(D) The refractive index is the ratio of speeds: $n = \\frac{c}{v} = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu\\varepsilon}{\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0}} = \\sqrt{\\mu_r\\varepsilon_r}$, so D matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":63,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The electric field amplitude of a plane electromagnetic wave in vacuum is 60 V/m. What is the average total energy density (electric plus magnetic) of the wave? (Take $\\varepsilon_0 = 8.85 \\times 10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["$1.59 \\times 10^{-8}$ J/m$^3$","$7.97 \\times 10^{-9}$ J/m$^3$","$3.19 \\times 10^{-8}$ J/m$^3$","$1.59 \\times 10^{-5}$ J/m$^3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $E_0 = 60$ V/m.\nThe average electric energy density is $\\frac{1}{4}\\varepsilon_0 E_0^2$. The average magnetic energy density $\\frac{B_0^2}{4\\mu_0}$ has the same value, since $B_0 = \\frac{E_0}{c}$.\nFormula: $u_{avg} = \\frac{1}{2}\\varepsilon_0 E_0^2$\nSubstituting: $u_{avg} = 0.5 \\times 8.85 \\times 10^{-12} \\times 3600$\nSo $u_{avg} \\approx 1.59 \\times 10^{-8}$ J/m$^3$.\nOption (2) is the electric part alone.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":64,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (scientist) with List-II (contribution).\nList-I:\n(A) Maxwell\n(B) Hertz\n(C) J.C. Bose\n(D) Marconi\nList-II:\n(I) Produced EM waves of wavelength 25 mm to 5 mm in a laboratory\n(II) Transmitted EM waves over many kilometres\n(III) Introduced displacement current and unified electricity, magnetism and light\n(IV) Produced EM waves, measured their wavelength and speed, and showed they reflect and refract like light","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Maxwell: removed the inconsistency in Ampere's law with displacement current and unified electricity, magnetism and light, so III.\n(B) Hertz: produced and sent EM waves, measured their wavelength and speed, and showed light-like reflection and refraction, so IV.\n(C) Bose: produced short waves of 25 mm to 5 mm in a laboratory, so I.\n(D) Marconi: transmitted waves over many kilometres, so II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":65,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of radiation) with List-II (method of production as given in NCERT).\nList-I:\n(A) Radio waves\n(B) Microwaves\n(C) Infrared\n(D) Gamma rays\nList-II:\n(P) Vibration of atoms and molecules\n(Q) Radioactive decay of the nucleus\n(R) Rapid acceleration and deceleration of electrons in aerials\n(S) Klystron or magnetron valve","image":"","options":["A-R, B-S, C-P, D-Q","A-S, B-R, C-P, D-Q","A-R, B-S, C-Q, D-P","A-P, B-S, C-R, D-Q"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Match each radiation with its source from Table 8.1:\n(A) Radio waves come from electrons accelerating in aerials, so A matches R.\n(B) Microwaves come from klystron or magnetron valves, so B matches S.\n(C) Infrared comes from vibration of atoms and molecules, so C matches P.\n(D) Gamma rays come from radioactive decay of the nucleus, so D matches Q.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":66,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"For a plane electromagnetic wave in vacuum, what is the ratio of the average energy density of the electric field to the average energy density of the magnetic field?","image":"","options":["1","c","$c^2$","$\\frac{1}{c}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Average electric energy density: $\\frac{1}{4}\\varepsilon_0 E_0^2$.\nAverage magnetic energy density: $\\frac{B_0^2}{4\\mu_0}$.\nPut $B_0 = \\frac{E_0}{c}$ and $c^2 = \\frac{1}{\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0}$. The magnetic energy density then becomes $\\frac{1}{4}\\varepsilon_0 E_0^2$.\nSo the two energy densities are equal, and the ratio is 1.\nOption (2) is tempting because $E_0/B_0 = c$, but that is a ratio of field amplitudes, not of energies.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":67,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Most of the ultraviolet radiation from the sun is absorbed by the ozone layer at an altitude of about 40–50 km.\nStatement II: Ordinary glass absorbs ultraviolet radiation, which is why welders use goggles with glass windows to protect their eyes.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. The ozone layer at about 40–50 km absorbs most of the sun's UV.\nStatement II is true. Ordinary glass absorbs UV. This is why you cannot get tanned through a window, and why glass goggles protect welders from UV produced by welding arcs.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":68,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A plane electromagnetic wave of frequency 50 MHz travels in vacuum. The amplitude of its electric field is 90 V/m. (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s.)\nStatement I: The wavelength of the wave is 60 m.\nStatement II: The amplitude of its magnetic field is $3 \\times 10^{-7}$ T.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I: $\\lambda = \\frac{c}{\\nu} = \\frac{3\\times10^{8}}{5\\times10^{7}} = 6$ m, not 60 m. So it is incorrect.\nStatement II: $B_0 = \\frac{E_0}{c} = \\frac{90}{3\\times10^{8}} = 3\\times10^{-7}$ T. So it is correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":69,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The electric field of an electromagnetic wave is given by $\\vec{E} = E_0\\sin(ky + \\omega t)\\,\\hat{i}$. In which direction does the wave travel?","image":"","options":["Along +y","Along −y","Along +x","Along +z"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The variable inside the sine, y, gives the axis of propagation. The field direction $\\hat{i}$ does not give it.\nA phase $(ky - \\omega t)$ describes a wave moving towards +y. The phase $(ky + \\omega t)$ stays constant only if y decreases as t increases, so this wave moves towards −y.\n+x is only the direction of $\\vec{E}$. An em wave is transverse, so it cannot travel along its own E.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":70,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) NCERT quotes visible light as spanning about $4 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz to $7 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz, with the lower-frequency end being red.\n(ii) Exposure to UV radiation reduces the production of melanin, and this causes tanning of the skin.\n(iii) The wavelength ranges quoted for X-rays and gamma rays overlap.\n(iv) Depletion of the ozone layer by CFCs such as freon would increase the UV reaching the earth's surface.\n(v) Every UV wavelength is longer than every X-ray wavelength.\n(vi) X-rays are used to treat certain cancers, but gamma rays have no use in medicine.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Visible light runs from about $4 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz (red, about 700 nm) to $7 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz (violet, about 400 nm).\n(ii) Incorrect: UV induces more melanin production, and that causes tanning.\n(iii) Correct: X-rays span $10^{-8}$–$10^{-13}$ m and gamma rays span $10^{-10}$ to below $10^{-14}$ m, so the ranges overlap.\n(iv) Correct: Ozone absorbs solar UV, so less ozone lets more UV reach the ground.\n(v) Incorrect: UV extends down to 0.6 nm, while X-rays begin at about 10 nm, so these ranges overlap too.\n(vi) Incorrect: Gamma rays are used in medicine to destroy cancer cells.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":71,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A charged particle executes simple harmonic motion with a time period of 2 ns. What is the frequency of the electromagnetic waves it radiates?","image":"","options":["$5 \\times 10^{8}$ Hz","$1 \\times 10^{9}$ Hz","$2.5 \\times 10^{8}$ Hz","$3.14 \\times 10^{9}$ Hz"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: T = 2 ns = $2 \\times 10^{-9}$ s.\nThe radiated wave has the same frequency as the oscillating charge, so $\\nu = \\frac{1}{T}$.\nSubstituting: $\\nu = \\frac{1}{2 \\times 10^{-9}}$.\nSo $\\nu = 5 \\times 10^{8}$ Hz.\nThe value $3.14 \\times 10^{9}$ is the angular frequency $\\frac{2\\pi}{T}$ in rad/s, not the frequency.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":72,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A parallel plate capacitor is being charged by a time-varying current $i(t)$.\nStatement I: In the region between the capacitor plates, there is no conduction current, and the displacement current equals $i$.\nStatement II: In the connecting wires outside the capacitor, the current is purely displacement current.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. No charges flow across the gap, so $i_c = 0$ there. The changing electric flux gives $i_d = \\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = i$.\nStatement II is incorrect. In the wires, charges actually flow, so $i_c = i$ and $i_d = 0$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":73,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A parallel plate capacitor is being charged by a time-varying current $i(t)$. How are the conduction current $i_c$ and the displacement current $i_d$ distributed between the connecting wire and the gap between the plates?","image":"","options":["In the wire, $i_c = i$ and $i_d = i$; in the gap, both are zero","In the wire, $i_c = i$ and $i_d = 0$; in the gap, $i_c = 0$ and $i_d = i$","In the wire, $i_c = 0$ and $i_d = i$; in the gap, $i_c = i$ and $i_d = 0$","In the wire, $i_c = i$ and $i_d = 0$; in the gap, $i_c = 0$ and $i_d = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Outside the plates, charges flow in the wire and the electric field there is not changing. So $i_c = i$ and $i_d = 0$.\nBetween the plates no charge crosses the gap, so $i_c = 0$.\nThe changing field in the gap gives $i_d = \\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = \\frac{dQ}{dt} = i$.\nThe total current is therefore $i$ everywhere. This is why B just inside the gap equals B just outside.\nOption (4) is the old inconsistency that Maxwell removed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":74,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Maxwell's equations in vacuum) with List-II (physical content).\nList-I:\n(A) $\\oint \\vec{E}\\cdot d\\vec{A} = Q/\\epsilon_0$\n(B) $\\oint \\vec{B}\\cdot d\\vec{A} = 0$\n(C) $\\oint \\vec{E}\\cdot d\\vec{l} = -\\frac{d\\Phi_B}{dt}$\n(D) $\\oint \\vec{B}\\cdot d\\vec{l} = \\mu_0 i_c + \\mu_0\\epsilon_0\\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$\nList-II:\n(I) A time-varying magnetic field gives rise to an electric field\n(II) Electric charge is the source of electric flux\n(III) A time-varying electric field is also a source of magnetic field\n(IV) Isolated magnetic poles do not exist","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A) Gauss's law for electricity: the enclosed charge determines the electric flux, so A-II.\n(B) Gauss's law for magnetism: the net flux is zero, so there are no monopoles, A-IV for B.\n(C) Faraday's law: a changing $\\Phi_B$ produces an emf, and hence an electric field, so C-I.\n(D) Ampere-Maxwell law: the term $\\mu_0\\epsilon_0\\, d\\Phi_E/dt$ shows that a changing E-field produces B, so D-III.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":75,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The electric flux through a surface lying between the plates of a charging capacitor increases at the rate of $2.26 \\times 10^{11}$ V m s$^{-1}$. Find the displacement current through the surface. (Take $\\varepsilon_0 = 8.85 \\times 10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["2.0 A","0.20 A","20 A","25.5 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = 2.26\\times10^{11}$ V m s$^{-1}$ and $\\varepsilon_0 = 8.85\\times10^{-12}$ SI units.\nFormula: $i_d = \\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$\nSubstituting: $i_d = 8.85\\times10^{-12} \\times 2.26\\times10^{11}$\nSo $i_d \\approx 2.0$ A\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":76,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Match each type of radiation in List-I with the accelerated charge or process that produces it in List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Radio waves\n(B) Infrared waves\n(C) X-rays\n(D) Gamma rays\nList-II:\n(P) Vibration of atoms and molecules\n(Q) Radioactive decay of the nucleus\n(R) Rapid acceleration and deceleration of electrons in aerials\n(S) High-energy electrons bombarding a metal target","image":"","options":["A-R, B-P, C-S, D-Q","A-P, B-R, C-S, D-Q","A-R, B-P, C-Q, D-S","A-S, B-P, C-R, D-Q"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-R: radio waves come from electrons accelerated back and forth in aerials.\nB-P: infrared comes from vibrating atoms and molecules, as in hot bodies.\nC-S: X-rays are produced when high-energy electrons are suddenly stopped by a metal target.\nD-Q: gamma rays originate in the nucleus, through radioactive decay.\nThe pattern shows that the radiated wavelength roughly follows the size of the radiating system.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":77,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A parallel plate capacitor has circular plates of radius 10 cm separated by 2.0 mm in air. It is charged by a constant current of 0.20 A. Find the rate at which the potential difference between the plates increases. (Take $\\varepsilon_0 = 8.85 \\times 10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["$1.44 \\times 10^{9}$ V/s","$2.88 \\times 10^{9}$ V/s","$7.19 \\times 10^{8}$ V/s","$1.44 \\times 10^{7}$ V/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $r = 0.10$ m, $d = 2.0\\times10^{-3}$ m, $i = 0.20$ A.\nFormula: $C = \\frac{\\varepsilon_0 \\pi r^2}{d}$. Since $i = C\\frac{dV}{dt}$, we get $\\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{i}{C}$.\nSubstituting: $C = \\frac{8.85\\times10^{-12} \\times 0.0314}{0.002} \\approx 1.39\\times10^{-10}$ F.\nThen $\\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{0.20}{1.39\\times10^{-10}}$.\nSo $\\frac{dV}{dt} \\approx 1.44\\times10^{9}$ V/s\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":78,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The electric field of a plane EM wave in vacuum is $E_x = 30 \\sin(kz - \\omega t)$ V/m, with $k = \\pi$ rad/m. What is the magnetic field $B_y$ at $z = 0.5$ m and $t = \\frac{1}{9}\\times10^{-8}$ s? (Take $c = 3\\times10^8$ m/s)","image":"","options":["$5.0\\times10^{-8}$ T","$8.66\\times10^{-8}$ T","$1.0\\times10^{-7}$ T","$2.5\\times10^{-8}$ T"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $E_0 = 30$ V/m, $k = \\pi$ rad/m\nFormula: $B_0 = \\frac{E_0}{c} = 10^{-7}$ T and $\\omega = ck = 3\\pi\\times10^8$ rad/s\nPhase: $kz = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\omega t = 3\\pi\\times10^8 \\times \\frac{10^{-8}}{9} = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$, so $kz - \\omega t = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$\nSubstituting: $B_y = 10^{-7}\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{6} = 10^{-7}\\times0.5$\nSo $B_y$ = $5.0\\times10^{-8}$ T. The fields are in phase, so B uses the same phase as E.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":79,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A capacitor is being charged by a time-varying current. A tiffin-box-shaped surface has its rim on a circular loop around the connecting wire, and its flat bottom S lies between the plates. Which quantity passes through the bottom S and makes the generalised Ampere's law consistent?","image":"","options":["A conduction current carried by electrons crossing the gap","A time-varying electric flux","A steady magnetic flux","A current due to positive ions moving between the plates"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"No charges cross the gap between the plates, so the conduction current through S is zero.\nThe electric field between the plates is perpendicular to S. Because the charge on the plates changes, the electric flux $\\Phi_E = \\frac{Q}{\\varepsilon_0}$ through S changes with time.\nThe term $\\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$ is the displacement current. It equals i, which removes the contradiction.\nOptions (1) and (4) fail because no charge actually flows across the gap.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":80,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A parallel plate capacitor is being charged by a steady current of 0.5 A. Find the rate of change of electric flux through a flat surface lying between the plates and completely covering the plate area. ($\\varepsilon_0 = 8.85\\times10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["$5.65\\times10^{10}$ V m s$^{-1}$","$4.43\\times10^{-12}$ V m s$^{-1}$","$1.77\\times10^{11}$ V m s$^{-1}$","$5.65\\times10^{9}$ V m s$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{dQ}{dt} = 0.5$ A and $\\varepsilon_0 = 8.85\\times10^{-12}$ SI units.\nFormula: $\\Phi_E = \\frac{Q}{\\varepsilon_0}$, so $\\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = \\frac{1}{\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{dQ}{dt}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = \\frac{0.5}{8.85\\times10^{-12}} \\approx 5.65\\times10^{10}$.\nSo $\\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = 5.65\\times10^{10}$ V m s$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":81,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"An oscillating charge radiates electromagnetic waves into vacuum. Where does the energy carried by these waves come from?","image":"","options":["From the energy of the source, that is, the accelerated charge","From the vacuum through which the wave travels","From the Earth's magnetic field","From nowhere, because electromagnetic waves carry no energy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the energy of the propagating wave comes at the expense of the energy of the source, the accelerated charge.\nVacuum has no material medium, so it cannot supply energy.\nThe Earth's field plays no role in the radiation.\nElectromagnetic waves do carry energy; sunlight bringing energy to the Earth is an example.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":82,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Maxwell concluded that light is an electromagnetic wave because the speed $c = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0 \\varepsilon_0}}$, obtained from purely electric and magnetic constants, came out very close to the speed of light measured optically.\nStatement II: Hertz verified Maxwell's theory by building an oscillating circuit whose charges oscillated at the frequency of visible light and detecting the light it emitted.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Maxwell's equations give a wave speed of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0 \\varepsilon_0}} \\approx 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s. This matched optical measurements, leading to the conclusion that light is an EM wave.\nStatement II is incorrect. Circuits can reach only about $10^{11}$ Hz, while visible light is about $10^{14}$ Hz.\nHertz instead worked in the radio region, with wavelengths of a few metres. He showed that these waves behave like light in reflection and refraction.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":83,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A long straight wire carries a current of 2 A to charge a capacitor. A circular loop of radius 4 cm is drawn with its plane perpendicular to the wire and its centre on the wire. Using Ampere's circuital law with a flat surface pierced by the wire, find the magnitude of the magnetic field on the loop. ($\\mu_0 = 4\\pi \\times 10^{-7}$ T m/A)","image":"","options":["10 μT","20 μT","5 μT","31.4 μT"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: i = 2 A, r = 0.04 m.\nFormula: by symmetry, $B(2\\pi r) = \\mu_0 i$, so $B = \\frac{\\mu_0 i}{2\\pi r}$.\nSubstituting: $B = \\frac{4\\pi\\times10^{-7}\\times 2}{2\\pi\\times0.04} = 1.0\\times10^{-5}$ T.\nSo B = 10 μT.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":84,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Take the highest frequency reachable by an electronic circuit as $10^{11}$ Hz and the frequency of yellow light as $6 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz. How many times longer is the wavelength radiated by such a circuit than the wavelength of yellow light? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s.)","image":"","options":["600","6000","60000","$6 \\times 10^{5}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\nu_1 = 10^{11}$ Hz (circuit) and $\\nu_2 = 6 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz (yellow light).\nFormula: $\\lambda = \\frac{c}{\\nu}$, so $\\frac{\\lambda_1}{\\lambda_2} = \\frac{\\nu_2}{\\nu_1}$.\nSubstituting: $\\lambda_1 = 3$ mm and $\\lambda_2 = 500$ nm, so the ratio is $\\frac{6 \\times 10^{14}}{10^{11}}$.\nSo the ratio is 6000.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":85,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about displacement current are correct?\n(i) It produces a magnetic field in exactly the same way as conduction current.\n(ii) It can exist only in vacuum, never in a material medium.\n(iii) For a charging capacitor, the displacement current between the plates equals the conduction current in the connecting wires.\n(iv) It is caused by charges physically flowing across the gap between the capacitor plates.\n(v) A magnetic field can exist in a region with no conduction current if the electric field there changes with time.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT states that displacement current has the same physical effects as conduction current.\n(ii) Incorrect: real media are neither perfect conductors nor perfect insulators, so both currents often coexist in the same region.\n(iii) Correct: inside the plates $i_d = i$.\n(iv) Incorrect: no charge crosses the gap; $i_d$ comes from a changing electric field.\n(v) Correct: a changing electric field alone produces a magnetic field.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":86,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"An FM radio station broadcasts electromagnetic waves of frequency 75 MHz. What is the wavelength of these waves in vacuum? (Take $c = 3\\times10^8$ m/s)","image":"","options":["4.0 m","0.25 m","40 m","2.5 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\nu = 75\\times10^6$ Hz, $c = 3\\times10^8$ m/s\nFormula: $\\nu\\lambda = c$, so $\\lambda = \\frac{c}{\\nu}$\nSubstituting: $\\lambda = \\frac{3\\times10^8}{75\\times10^6}$\nSo $\\lambda$ = 4.0 m\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":87,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about electromagnetic waves are correct?\n(i) In a plane EM wave in vacuum, the electric and magnetic fields oscillate in phase.\n(ii) The amplitudes of the fields satisfy $\\frac{E_0}{B_0} = c$.\n(iii) Gamma rays travel faster than radio waves in vacuum because they have a higher frequency.\n(iv) $\\oint \\vec{B} \\cdot d\\vec{A} = 0$ reflects the fact that no magnetic monopoles are known.\n(v) In a medium of permittivity $\\varepsilon$ and permeability $\\mu$, the speed of the wave is $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu \\varepsilon}}$.\n(vi) A charge oscillating at frequency $\\nu$ radiates EM waves of frequency $2\\nu$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $E_x$ and $B_y$ both vary as $\\sin(kz - \\omega t)$.\n(ii) Correct: $B_0 = \\frac{E_0}{c}$.\n(iii) Incorrect: all EM waves travel at the same speed $c$ in vacuum.\n(iv) Correct: the zero net magnetic flux expresses the absence of magnetic monopoles.\n(v) Correct: $v = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu \\varepsilon}}$.\n(vi) Incorrect: the wave has the same frequency $\\nu$ as the oscillating charge.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":88,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Take the visible spectrum to extend from 400 nm to 700 nm in vacuum. What is the width of the visible band expressed in frequency, i.e. $\\nu_{violet} - \\nu_{red}$? ($c = 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s)","image":"","options":["$3.2 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz","$1.8 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz","$11.8 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz","$4.3 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\lambda_{violet} = 400$ nm, $\\lambda_{red} = 700$ nm, $c = 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s.\nFormula: $\\nu = \\frac{c}{\\lambda}$.\nSubstituting: $\\nu_{violet} = \\frac{3 \\times 10^8}{4 \\times 10^{-7}} = 7.5 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz and $\\nu_{red} = \\frac{3 \\times 10^8}{7 \\times 10^{-7}} \\approx 4.29 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz.\nSo the band width = $7.5 \\times 10^{14} - 4.29 \\times 10^{14} \\approx 3.2 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz.\nOption (4) is only the red-edge frequency, not the width.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":89,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Most of the ultraviolet radiation coming from the sun is absorbed before it reaches the ground. Where does this absorption mainly take place?","image":"","options":["In the ionosphere at about 300 km altitude","In the troposphere, close to the earth's surface","In the ozone layer at an altitude of about 40–50 km","In clouds of water vapour at about 2–5 km"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the sun is an important source of UV.\nMost of this UV is absorbed by the ozone layer, at an altitude of about 40–50 km.\nThis protective role is why the depletion of ozone by CFCs is a matter of international concern.\nThe other layers and altitudes listed are not the main absorbers of solar UV.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":90,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Light travels more slowly in glass than in vacuum.\nReason (R): In a material medium, $\\varepsilon$ and $\\mu$ replace $\\varepsilon_0$ and $\\mu_0$ in Maxwell's equations, and for glass the product $\\mu\\varepsilon$ is greater than $\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: light travels more slowly in glass than in vacuum.\nR is true: in a medium, $v = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu\\varepsilon}}$. For glass, $\\varepsilon > \\varepsilon_0$ while $\\mu \\approx \\mu_0$, so $\\mu\\varepsilon > \\mu_0\\varepsilon_0$.\nA larger $\\mu\\varepsilon$ gives $v < c$, so R directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":91,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A plane electromagnetic wave in vacuum has $E_x = E_0 \\sin(kz - \\omega t)$. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The wave propagates along the +z direction.\n(ii) Its speed of propagation is $\\frac{\\omega}{k}$.\n(iii) Its wavelength is $\\frac{2\\pi}{k}$.\n(iv) Its magnetic field oscillates along the z-axis.\n(v) Its frequency in hertz equals $\\omega$.\n(vi) The direction of the wave vector $\\vec{k}$ is the direction of $\\vec{E} \\times \\vec{B}$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: a phase of the form $(kz - \\omega t)$ means the wave moves toward +z.\n(ii) Correct: the wave speed is $\\frac{\\omega}{k} = c$.\n(iii) Correct: $k = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\lambda}$, so $\\lambda = \\frac{2\\pi}{k}$.\n(iv) Incorrect: B is transverse and lies along y.\n(v) Incorrect: $\\omega$ is the angular frequency. The frequency is $\\nu = \\frac{\\omega}{2\\pi}$.\n(vi) Correct: $\\hat{i} \\times \\hat{j} = \\hat{k}$, which matches the propagation direction.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":92,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The electric field of a plane electromagnetic wave in vacuum is $E_x = 90 \\sin(20\\pi z - \\omega t)$ V/m, where z is in metres. What is the frequency $\\nu$ of the wave? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s)","image":"","options":["3.0 GHz","18.85 GHz","1.5 GHz","6.0 GHz"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $k = 20\\pi$ rad/m, $c = 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s.\nFormula: $\\omega = ck$ and $\\nu = \\frac{\\omega}{2\\pi} = \\frac{ck}{2\\pi}$.\nSubstituting: $\\nu = \\frac{3 \\times 10^8 \\times 20\\pi}{2\\pi} = 3 \\times 10^9$ Hz.\nSo $\\nu = 3.0$ GHz. The value 18.85 is $\\omega$ in units of $10^9$ rad/s, not the frequency.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":93,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A plane electromagnetic wave travels in vacuum. The amplitude of its electric field is 90 V/m. What is the amplitude of its magnetic field? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s)","image":"","options":["300 nT","30 nT","3000 nT","0.3 nT"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $E_0 = 90$ V/m, $c = 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s.\nFormula: In vacuum, $B_0 = \\frac{E_0}{c}$.\nSubstituting: $B_0 = \\frac{90}{3 \\times 10^8} = 3 \\times 10^{-7}$ T.\nSince $1$ nT $= 10^{-9}$ T, $B_0 = 300$ nT.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":94,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (broadcast band) with List-II (frequency range).\nList-I:\n(A) AM radio\n(B) Short wave bands\n(C) TV waves\n(D) FM radio\nList-II:\n(i) 88 MHz to 108 MHz\n(ii) 54 MHz to 890 MHz\n(iii) 530 kHz to 1710 kHz\n(iv) higher frequencies up to 54 MHz","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) AM band: 530–1710 kHz, so A matches (iii).\n(B) Short-wave bands: higher frequencies up to 54 MHz, so B matches (iv).\n(C) TV waves: 54–890 MHz, so C matches (ii).\n(D) FM band: 88–108 MHz, so D matches (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":95,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A transmitting antenna radiates most efficiently at a wavelength roughly equal to its own size. Estimate the ratio of the antenna size suitable for an AM station broadcasting at 600 kHz to that for an FM station broadcasting at 100 MHz.","image":"","options":["about 167","about 16.7","about 1.67","about $6 \\times 10^{-3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\nu_{AM} = 600$ kHz and $\\nu_{FM} = 100$ MHz. The antenna size is about $\\lambda$.\nFormula: $\\frac{L_{AM}}{L_{FM}} \\approx \\frac{\\lambda_{AM}}{\\lambda_{FM}} = \\frac{\\nu_{FM}}{\\nu_{AM}}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{100\\times10^{6}}{600\\times10^{3}} \\approx 167$.\nAs a check, $\\lambda_{AM} = 500$ m and $\\lambda_{FM} = 3$ m.\nThe value $6\\times10^{-3}$ is the inverted ratio.\nSo the AM antenna must be about 167 times larger.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":96,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"In a region of space, the electric flux through a surface is changing at a rate of $2 \\times 10^{10}$ V m/s. What is the displacement current through the surface? (Take $\\varepsilon_0 = 8.85 \\times 10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["0.177 A","1.77 A","0.0885 A","17.7 A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = 2 \\times 10^{10}$ V m/s and $\\varepsilon_0 = 8.85 \\times 10^{-12}$ SI units.\nFormula: $i_d = \\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$\nSubstituting: $i_d = 8.85 \\times 10^{-12} \\times 2 \\times 10^{10}$\nSo $i_d = 0.177$ A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":97,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following pairings of an application with the radiation it uses are correct?\n(i) Microwave oven – microwaves matched to the resonant frequency of water molecules\n(ii) Germ-killing lamps in water purifiers – ultraviolet\n(iii) TV remote switches – infrared\n(iv) Destroying cancer cells – gamma rays\n(v) LASIK eye surgery – X-rays\n(vi) Aircraft navigation radar – infrared","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: microwaves transfer energy to water molecules.\n(ii) Correct: UV lamps kill germs.\n(iii) Correct: LEDs in remote controls emit infrared.\n(iv) Correct: gamma rays are used to destroy cancer cells.\n(v) Incorrect: LASIK uses narrow UV beams, not X-rays.\n(vi) Incorrect: radar uses microwaves, not infrared.\nSo 4 pairings are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":98,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"For a plane electromagnetic wave in vacuum described by $E_x = E_0 \\sin(kz - \\omega t)$ and $B_y = B_0 \\sin(kz - \\omega t)$, match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) $k$\n(B) $\\omega$\n(C) $\\frac{\\omega}{k}$\n(D) $B_0$\nList-II:\n(i) $\\frac{E_0}{c}$\n(ii) $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\lambda}$\n(iii) $2\\pi\\nu$\n(iv) $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0 \\varepsilon_0}}$","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The magnitude of the wave vector is $k = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\lambda}$, so A-(ii).\n(B) Angular frequency is $\\omega = 2\\pi\\nu$, so B-(iii).\n(C) The wave speed is $\\frac{\\omega}{k} = c = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0 \\varepsilon_0}}$, so C-(iv).\n(D) The field amplitudes are related by $B_0 = \\frac{E_0}{c}$, so D-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":99,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The magnetic field of a plane electromagnetic wave in vacuum is $\\vec{B} = 3 \\times 10^{-8} \\sin(2.0 \\times 10^{2} x - 6.0 \\times 10^{10} t)\\,\\hat{k}$ T, with x in metres and t in seconds. Which expression correctly gives the electric field of the wave? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s)","image":"","options":["$\\vec{E} = 9 \\sin(2.0 \\times 10^{2} x - 6.0 \\times 10^{10} t)\\,\\hat{j}$ V/m","$\\vec{E} = -9 \\sin(2.0 \\times 10^{2} x - 6.0 \\times 10^{10} t)\\,\\hat{j}$ V/m","$\\vec{E} = 9 \\sin(2.0 \\times 10^{2} x - 6.0 \\times 10^{10} t)\\,\\hat{i}$ V/m","$\\vec{E} = 90 \\sin(2.0 \\times 10^{2} x - 6.0 \\times 10^{10} t)\\,\\hat{j}$ V/m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The argument is $(kx - \\omega t)$, so the wave travels along +x. As a check, $\\frac{\\omega}{k} = \\frac{6 \\times 10^{10}}{2 \\times 10^2} = 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s.\nAmplitude: $E_0 = B_0 c = 3 \\times 10^{-8} \\times 3 \\times 10^8 = 9$ V/m.\nDirection: $\\vec{E} \\times \\vec{B}$ must point along $+\\hat{i}$. With B along $+\\hat{k}$, we need $\\hat{j} \\times \\hat{k} = \\hat{i}$, so E is along $+\\hat{j}$.\nE and B are in phase, so there is no minus sign. Option (3) fails because E cannot lie along the propagation direction.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":100,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A person sitting in a closed room behind ordinary glass windows does not get sunburnt or tanned, even in strong sunlight.\nReason (R): Ordinary glass absorbs ultraviolet radiation.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Tanning and sunburn are caused by UV radiation, which makes the skin produce more melanin.\nR is true. NCERT states that ordinary glass absorbs UV radiation.\nGlass blocks the UV that would cause tanning. So R directly explains why no tanning or sunburn happens through glass windows.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":101,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The electric field of a plane electromagnetic wave in vacuum is $E_y = 60\\sin(20\\pi x - \\omega t)$ V/m, where x is in metres. What is the wavelength of the wave?","image":"","options":["10 cm","5 cm","20 cm","3.18 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the wave is written as $\\sin(kx - \\omega t)$, so $k = 20\\pi$ rad/m.\nFormula: $\\lambda = \\frac{2\\pi}{k}$.\nSubstituting: $\\lambda = \\frac{2\\pi}{20\\pi} = 0.1$ m.\nSo $\\lambda$ = 10 cm. The 5 cm option forgets the factor 2 in $2\\pi$. The 3.18 cm option wrongly drops $\\pi$ from k and takes $\\frac{1}{20}$ m divided by $\\pi$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":102,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(P) Microwaves\n(Q) Infrared waves\n(R) Ultraviolet rays\n(S) X-rays\nList-II\n(1) Mostly absorbed by the ozone layer at an altitude of about 40-50 km\n(2) Commonly produced by bombarding a metal target with high-energy electrons\n(3) Frequency chosen to match the resonant frequency of water molecules when used in ovens\n(4) Re-radiated by the earth's surface and trapped by greenhouse gases","image":"","options":["P-4, Q-3, R-1, S-2","P-3, Q-4, R-2, S-1","P-3, Q-4, R-1, S-2","P-1, Q-4, R-3, S-2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Microwave ovens tune the frequency to the resonant frequency of water molecules: P-3.\nThe earth re-radiates absorbed sunlight as infrared, which CO$_2$ and water vapour trap: Q-4.\nThe ozone layer absorbs most of the sun's UV: R-1.\nX-rays are generated by firing high-energy electrons at a metal target: S-2.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":103,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A transmitting antenna about 3 m long is well suited to radiate efficiently in the FM radio band.\nStatement II: Visible light emitted by atoms has a wavelength comparable to the size of an atom.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An antenna radiates most efficiently at wavelengths close to its own size.\nStatement I: for $\\lambda \\approx 3$ m, $\\nu = \\frac{c}{\\lambda} = \\frac{3\\times10^{8}}{3} = 100$ MHz. This lies in the FM band (88–108 MHz), so Statement I is correct.\nStatement II: atoms are about $10^{-10}$ m across, but visible light is 400–700 nm. NCERT notes visible light is much longer than atomic size, so Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":104,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A plane electromagnetic wave travels in free space along the negative y-direction. At a certain point and instant, its electric field points along $+\\hat{k}$. In which direction does the magnetic field point at that point and instant?","image":"","options":["$+\\hat{i}$","$-\\hat{i}$","$+\\hat{j}$","$-\\hat{k}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$\\vec{E} \\times \\vec{B}$ must point along the direction of propagation, $-\\hat{j}$.\nB must also be perpendicular to both E (along z) and the propagation direction (along y), so it lies along the x-axis.\nCheck $+\\hat{i}$: $\\hat{k} \\times \\hat{i} = +\\hat{j}$. This is wrong, since it gives propagation along +y.\nCheck $-\\hat{i}$: $\\hat{k} \\times (-\\hat{i}) = -\\hat{j}$. This is correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":105,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A parallel plate capacitor is being charged by a time-varying current.\nStatement I: If only conduction current is counted, Kirchhoff's junction rule appears to fail at each plate, because current flows into a plate but no conduction current leaves it across the gap.\nStatement II: If the displacement current between the plates is included, the current entering a plate equals the current leaving it.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Conduction current $i$ reaches the plate through the wire, but no charge crosses the gap. Counting conduction current alone, the junction rule seems violated.\nStatement II is correct. Between the plates there is a displacement current $i_d = \\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = i$. With it included, the total current is continuous and the junction rule holds at each plate.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":106,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A capacitor with plates of area A carries charge Q and is being charged by current i. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Flat disc bounded by the loop and pierced by the wire\n(b) Tiffin-shaped surface with bottom S between the plates\n(c) Magnitude of electric field between the plates\n(d) Electric flux through S\nList-II\n(i) $\\frac{Q}{\\varepsilon_0}$\n(ii) $\\frac{Q}{\\varepsilon_0 A}$\n(iii) Conduction current i, displacement current zero\n(iv) Conduction current zero, displacement current i","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) The wire crosses the disc, and the field there is not changing. So $i_c = i$ and $i_d = 0$, which matches (iii).\n(b) Only a changing E-field crosses S. So $i_c = 0$ and $i_d = i$, which matches (iv).\n(c) The field between the plates is $E = \\frac{Q/A}{\\varepsilon_0}$, which matches (ii).\n(d) The flux is $\\Phi_E = EA = \\frac{Q}{\\varepsilon_0}$, which matches (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":107,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A plane electromagnetic wave in vacuum has electric field $E_x = 90\\sin(kz - \\omega t)$ V/m. What is the amplitude of its magnetic field? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s.)","image":"","options":["300 nT","30 nT","3000 nT","270 nT"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $E_0 = 90$ V/m and $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s.\nFormula: $B_0 = \\frac{E_0}{c}$.\nSubstituting: $B_0 = \\frac{90}{3 \\times 10^{8}} = 3 \\times 10^{-7}$ T.\nSo $B_0$ = 300 nT. Note that 1 nT = $10^{-9}$ T, so $3 \\times 10^{-7}$ T is 300 nT, not 30 nT or 3000 nT.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":108,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about a plane electromagnetic wave in vacuum are correct?\n(i) The electric and magnetic fields oscillate in phase.\n(ii) The field amplitudes are related by $B_0 = E_0 c$.\n(iii) X-rays and radio waves have the same speed.\n(iv) The wave transports energy from one place to another.\n(v) In a medium of relative permittivity greater than 1 (with $\\mu_r = 1$), the wave travels faster than c.","image":"","options":["3","2","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $E_x$ and $B_y$ both vary as $\\sin(kz - \\omega t)$, so they are in phase.\n(ii) Incorrect: the correct relation is $B_0 = \\frac{E_0}{c}$.\n(iii) Correct: all em waves travel at the same speed c in vacuum.\n(iv) Correct: em waves carry energy, for example sunlight reaching the earth.\n(v) Incorrect: $v = \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_r}} < c$.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":109,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A parallel plate capacitor has plate area 0.10 m$^2$ and plate separation 1.0 mm, with vacuum between the plates. It is connected to a source whose voltage is $V = 200\\sin(1000t)$ volt, where $t$ is in seconds. Find the peak value of the displacement current between the plates. (Take $\\epsilon_0 = 8.85 \\times 10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["0.0177 mA","0.177 mA","1.77 mA","0.354 mA"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $A = 0.10$ m$^2$, $d = 1.0\\times10^{-3}$ m, $V_0 = 200$ V, $\\omega = 1000$ rad s$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\Phi_E = EA = \\frac{VA}{d}$, so $i_d = \\epsilon_0\\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = \\frac{\\epsilon_0 A}{d}\\frac{dV}{dt} = C\\frac{dV}{dt}$. The peak value is $C V_0\\omega$.\nSubstituting: $C = \\frac{8.85\\times10^{-12}\\times0.10}{10^{-3}} = 8.85\\times10^{-10}$ F, so $i_{d,max} = 8.85\\times10^{-10}\\times200\\times1000 = 1.77\\times10^{-4}$ A\nSo the peak displacement current = 0.177 mA.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":110,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about displacement current are correct?\n(i) It exists only where the electric field changes with time.\n(ii) In a wire carrying a steady current, the displacement current is zero.\n(iii) It is caused by charges physically flowing across the gap of a capacitor.\n(iv) It produces a magnetic field in exactly the same way as a conduction current.\n(v) In a charging capacitor, the displacement current in the gap is smaller than the conduction current in the leads.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $i_d = \\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$ is non-zero only if the electric flux changes.\n(ii) Correct: for a steady current, E does not change with time, so $i_d = 0$.\n(iii) Incorrect: no charge crosses the gap; $i_d$ arises from the changing field.\n(iv) Correct: NCERT states it is a source of magnetic field just like conduction current.\n(v) Incorrect: the two currents are equal ($i_d = i$).\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":111,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"J.C. Bose, working in Calcutta, produced electromagnetic waves with wavelengths from 25 mm down to 5 mm. Taking $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s, what is the highest frequency in this range?","image":"","options":["12 GHz","60 GHz","6 GHz","120 GHz"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: shortest wavelength $\\lambda = 5$ mm $= 5 \\times 10^{-3}$ m, and $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s.\nFormula: $\\nu = \\frac{c}{\\lambda}$. The highest frequency corresponds to the shortest wavelength.\nSubstituting: $\\nu = \\frac{3 \\times 10^{8}}{5 \\times 10^{-3}} = 6 \\times 10^{10}$ Hz.\nSo the highest frequency = 60 GHz. The value 12 GHz is the lowest frequency, from 25 mm.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":112,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Who first succeeded in transmitting electromagnetic waves over distances of many kilometres, starting the field of communication using these waves?","image":"","options":["Heinrich Hertz","Jagdish Chandra Bose","Guglielmo Marconi","James Clerk Maxwell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Marconi followed Hertz's work in Italy and transmitted electromagnetic waves over many kilometres. NCERT says his experiment marks the beginning of communication using EM waves.\nHertz's and Bose's experiments were confined to the laboratory.\nMaxwell made the theoretical prediction but did no transmission.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":113,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Hertz showed that the vibration, reflection and refraction of his waves were of the same nature as those of light and heat waves.\n(ii) Bose's experiment sent electromagnetic waves over distances of many kilometres.\n(iii) Maxwell's work brought electricity, magnetism and light into a single framework.\n(iv) The waves produced by Hertz had shorter wavelengths than those produced by Bose.\n(v) Maxwell was led to displacement current by an inconsistency he found while applying Ampere's circuital law to a point outside a capacitor carrying a time-varying current.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is how Hertz first established that his waves and light are of the same nature.\n(ii) Incorrect: Bose's experiment, like Hertz's, was confined to the laboratory. Long-distance transmission was Marconi's work.\n(iii) Correct: Maxwell unified electricity, magnetism and light.\n(iv) Incorrect: Hertz's waves were a few metres long, much longer than Bose's 25 mm to 5 mm waves.\n(v) Correct: the capacitor contradiction led Maxwell to displacement current.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":114,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A person sitting behind a closed glass window in strong sunlight does not get a sun-tan.\nReason (R): Ordinary glass absorbs ultraviolet radiation.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that one cannot get tanned or sunburnt through glass windows.\nR is true: ordinary glass absorbs UV radiation.\nTanning happens because UV exposure makes the skin produce more melanin. If glass absorbs the UV, the radiation that causes tanning never reaches the skin, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":115,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"According to the NCERT summary of electromagnetic waves (Table 8.1), infrared radiation is detected by","image":"","options":["point contact diodes","thermopiles and bolometers","Geiger tubes and ionisation chambers","receiver's aerials"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Table 8.1 lists thermopiles, bolometers and infrared photographic film as detectors of infrared.\nThis fits because infrared is absorbed and heats the detector.\nPoint contact diodes detect microwaves.\nGeiger tubes and ionisation chambers detect X-rays and γ-rays.\nReceiver's aerials detect radio waves.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":116,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about Maxwell's equations in vacuum is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Gauss's law for magnetism states that the net magnetic flux through any closed surface is zero.","Faraday's law can be restated as: a magnetic field changing with time gives rise to an electric field.","The Ampere-Maxwell law allows a magnetic field to exist in a region where there is no conduction current.","The laws of electricity and magnetism become perfectly symmetric, because magnetic monopoles act as sources of magnetic field just as charges act as sources of electric field."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: $\\oint \\vec{B}\\cdot d\\vec{A} = 0$.\n(2) Correct: an induced emf implies an induced electric field.\n(3) Correct: the displacement current alone can produce a magnetic field.\n(4) Incorrect: NCERT notes that the laws are still not perfectly symmetric. No magnetic monopoles are known, so nothing acts as a source of B the way charges act as sources of E.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":117,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Infrared waves are often referred to as heat waves.\nReason (R): The infrared band lies adjacent to the long-wavelength end of the visible spectrum.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT does call infrared waves heat waves.\nR is true: infrared lies just beyond red, at the low-frequency end of the visible band.\nR is not the explanation, though. The name comes from absorption. Water, CO$_2$, NH$_3$ and similar molecules readily absorb infrared, which sets entire atoms and molecules vibrating. This raises their thermal motion and temperature.\nThe position of infrared in the spectrum has nothing to do with this heating.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":118,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"An electromagnetic wave travels through a non-magnetic medium ($\\mu_r = 1$) whose relative permittivity is $\\varepsilon_r = 4$. What is the speed of the wave in this medium? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s)","image":"","options":["$1.5 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s","$0.75 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s","$6 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s","$3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\varepsilon_r = 4$, $\\mu_r = 1$.\nFormula: $v = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu\\varepsilon}} = \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{\\mu_r\\varepsilon_r}}$\nSubstituting: $v = \\frac{3 \\times 10^{8}}{\\sqrt{4}}$\nSo $v = 1.5 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s.\nOption (2) comes from dividing by $\\varepsilon_r$ instead of $\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_r}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":119,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following will radiate electromagnetic waves?\n(i) An electron at rest\n(ii) An electron moving with constant velocity\n(iii) An electron moving in a circle with constant speed\n(iv) Electrons oscillating in a transmitting antenna\n(v) A fast electron being suddenly decelerated as it strikes a metal target\n(vi) A proton moving with constant velocity","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only accelerated charges radiate.\n(i) and (vi) do not radiate: zero velocity or constant velocity means zero acceleration.\n(ii) does not radiate: uniform motion gives no radiation.\n(iii) radiates: the direction of velocity keeps changing, so there is centripetal acceleration.\n(iv) radiates: oscillating charges are accelerated.\n(v) radiates: deceleration is acceleration. This is how X-rays are produced.\nThree items radiate.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":120,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"An FM radio station broadcasts at a frequency of 100 MHz. What is the wavelength of these waves in vacuum? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s)","image":"","options":["3 m","0.33 m","30 m","300 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\nu = 100$ MHz $= 10^{8}$ Hz and $c = 3\\times10^{8}$ m/s.\nFormula: $\\lambda = \\frac{c}{\\nu}$.\nSubstituting: $\\lambda = \\frac{3\\times10^{8}}{10^{8}}$.\nSo $\\lambda = 3$ m. This is well above 0.1 m, so it lies in the radio region.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":121,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A parallel plate capacitor has circular plates of radius 5.0 cm and capacitance 200 pF. It is connected to a 200 V (rms) ac supply of angular frequency 500 rad/s. What is the amplitude of the magnetic field at a point between the plates, 2.0 cm from the axis? (Take $\\mu_0 = 4\\pi \\times 10^{-7}$ T m/A and neglect edge effects.)","image":"","options":["$4.53 \\times 10^{-11}$ T","$3.20 \\times 10^{-11}$ T","$11.3 \\times 10^{-11}$ T","$28.3 \\times 10^{-11}$ T"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: R = 0.05 m, r = 0.02 m, C = 200 pF, $V_{rms}$ = 200 V, ω = 500 rad/s.\nFormula: $I_{rms} = \\omega C V_{rms}$ and $I_0 = \\sqrt{2}\\, I_{rms}$. Only the fraction $\\frac{r^2}{R^2}$ of the displacement current passes inside the loop, so $B(2\\pi r) = \\mu_0 I_0 \\frac{r^2}{R^2}$, giving $B_0 = \\frac{\\mu_0 I_0 r}{2\\pi R^2}$.\nSubstituting: $I_{rms} = 500 \\times 2 \\times 10^{-10} \\times 200 = 2 \\times 10^{-5}$ A, so $I_0 = 2.83 \\times 10^{-5}$ A.\nThen $B_0 = \\frac{2 \\times 10^{-7} \\times 2.83 \\times 10^{-5} \\times 0.02}{0.0025}$.\nSo $B_0 = 4.53 \\times 10^{-11}$ T.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":122,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A parallel plate capacitor (plate area A, charge Q) is being charged by a current i. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Electric flux through a surface between the plates\n(B) Displacement current between the plates\n(C) Current through a pot-shaped surface whose bottom lies between the plates, as per the original Ampere's law\n(D) Total current through any surface bounded by the loop, as per the Ampere-Maxwell law\nList-II\n(I) $\\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$\n(II) $i_c + \\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$\n(III) $\\frac{Q}{\\varepsilon_0}$\n(IV) Zero","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $\\Phi_E = EA = \\frac{Q}{\\varepsilon_0 A} \\cdot A = \\frac{Q}{\\varepsilon_0}$, so A matches III.\n(B) $i_d = \\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$, so B matches I.\n(C) No conduction current pierces the pot-shaped surface, so the original law counts zero current. C matches IV.\n(D) The Ampere-Maxwell law counts the total current $i_c + i_d$, so D matches II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":123,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Gamma radiation of wavelength about $10^{-14}$ m typically originates from an atomic nucleus.\nReason (R): The wavelength of electromagnetic radiation is often correlated with the characteristic size of the system that radiates it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that γ-rays of wavelength $10^{-14}$ to $10^{-15}$ m typically come from the nucleus.\nR is true: emitted wavelength is often correlated with the size of the radiating system.\nA nucleus is about $10^{-14}$ m across, which matches the γ-ray wavelength.\nSo R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":124,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The electric field of a plane electromagnetic wave in vacuum is $E_y = 45\\sin(4.0\\times10^{2}\\,x - 1.2\\times10^{11}\\,t)$ V/m, where x is in metres and t in seconds. What is the wavelength of this wave?","image":"","options":["1.57 cm","0.25 cm","3.14 cm","0.79 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the wave has the form $E_0\\sin(kx - \\omega t)$, so $k = 4.0\\times10^{2}$ rad/m and $\\omega = 1.2\\times10^{11}$ rad/s.\nFormula: $\\lambda = \\frac{2\\pi}{k}$.\nSubstituting: $\\lambda = \\frac{2\\pi}{400}$ m $= 1.57\\times10^{-2}$ m.\nCheck: $\\frac{\\omega}{k} = 3\\times10^{8}$ m/s $= c$, so the expression describes a wave in vacuum.\nSo $\\lambda$ = 1.57 cm. The option 0.25 cm comes from wrongly using $\\frac{1}{k}$. The option 3.14 cm comes from wrongly using $\\frac{4\\pi}{k}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":125,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following will NOT radiate electromagnetic waves?","image":"","options":["An electron oscillating harmonically about a mean position","A proton moving along a circle with constant speed","An electron moving in a straight line with constant velocity","A fast electron being suddenly stopped by a metal target"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: according to Maxwell's theory, only accelerated charges radiate electromagnetic waves.\n(1) An oscillating charge is continuously accelerated, so it radiates.\n(2) Uniform circular motion has centripetal acceleration, so it radiates. 'Constant speed' is the tempting trap here.\n(3) A charge with constant velocity has zero acceleration. It produces only a steady magnetic field, so it does not radiate.\n(4) A charge that decelerates sharply radiates. This is how X-rays are produced in an X-ray tube.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":126,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A parallel plate capacitor with circular plates of radius $R$ is charged by a constant current $I$. Edge effects are negligible. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The displacement current through the region between the plates equals $I$.\n(ii) Between the plates, the magnetic field at a distance $r < R$ from the axis is proportional to $r$.\n(iii) At a point just outside the plates, at distance $r > R$ from the axis, the magnetic field is $\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{2\\pi r}$.\n(iv) The electric field between the plates remains constant in time.\n(v) The magnetic field between the plates is maximum on the axis.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $i_d = \\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = \\frac{dQ}{dt} = I$.\n(ii) Correct: the displacement current is spread uniformly over the plate area. A loop of radius r encloses $I\\frac{r^2}{R^2}$, so $B = \\frac{\\mu_0 I r}{2\\pi R^2}$.\n(iii) Correct: such a loop encloses the full current $I$, matching the field near the wire.\n(iv) Incorrect: Q grows steadily, so E increases with time. A changing E is exactly what gives a non-zero $i_d$.\n(v) Incorrect: B is zero on the axis.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":127,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a capacitor being charged, Kirchhoff's junction rule holds at each plate, provided the displacement current is counted along with the conduction current.\nReason (R): The conduction current flowing into a plate is equal to the displacement current across the gap between the plates.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"If only conduction current is counted, current enters a plate but none leaves through the gap. The junction rule then appears to fail.\nThe displacement current in the gap is $\\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = \\frac{dQ}{dt}$, which equals the conduction current in the lead. So R is true.\nBecause the current into the plate equals the current out of it, the total current is continuous. The junction rule therefore holds, so A is true.\nR directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":128,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A parallel plate capacitor with circular plates of radius R is charged by a current i. A coaxial loop of radius r > R lies in the gap, parallel to the plates.\nStatement I: The magnetic field on this loop has the same magnitude as the field at distance r from the connecting wire outside the capacitor.\nStatement II: For the flat disc bounded by this loop, the displacement current increases if the plate separation is decreased while i is kept fixed.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. For r ≥ R the disc captures the whole flux $\\frac{Q}{\\varepsilon_0}$, so $i_d = \\varepsilon_0\\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = \\frac{dQ}{dt} = i$. This gives $B = \\frac{\\mu_0 i}{2\\pi r}$, the same as outside.\nStatement II is incorrect. Since $\\Phi_E = \\frac{Q}{\\varepsilon_0}$ does not involve the separation, $i_d = i$ regardless of the gap.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":129,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Electrons in a transmitting antenna oscillate as $x = A \\sin(\\omega t)$ with $\\omega = 4\\pi \\times 10^{8}$ rad/s. An antenna radiates most efficiently when its size is about the same as the radiated wavelength. What antenna length is best suited? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s.)","image":"","options":["0.24 m","0.75 m","1.5 m","3.0 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\omega = 4\\pi \\times 10^{8}$ rad/s.\nThe radiated frequency equals the oscillation frequency: $\\nu = \\frac{\\omega}{2\\pi} = 2 \\times 10^{8}$ Hz.\nFormula: $\\lambda = \\frac{c}{\\nu}$.\nSubstituting: $\\lambda = \\frac{3 \\times 10^{8}}{2 \\times 10^{8}} = 1.5$ m.\nSo the antenna length should be about 1.5 m.\nUsing $\\omega$ in place of $\\nu$ wrongly gives 0.24 m.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":130,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A plane electromagnetic wave travels in vacuum along the −y direction. At a certain point and instant, its magnetic field is $\\vec{B} = 4\\times10^{-8}\\,\\hat{i}$ T. What is the electric field at that point and instant? (Take $c = 3\\times10^8$ m/s)","image":"","options":["$-12\\,\\hat{k}$ V/m","$+12\\,\\hat{k}$ V/m","$-1.33\\times10^{-16}\\,\\hat{k}$ V/m","$+12\\,\\hat{j}$ V/m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Magnitude: $E = cB = 3\\times10^8 \\times 4\\times10^{-8} = 12$ V/m.\nDirection: $\\vec{E}$ must be perpendicular to both $\\hat{i}$ and the propagation axis (y), so it lies along $\\pm\\hat{k}$.\nThe direction of $\\vec{E}\\times\\vec{B}$ must be −y. Check: $(-\\hat{k})\\times\\hat{i} = -\\hat{j}$. This works.\n$+\\hat{k}$ gives propagation along +y. The value $1.33\\times10^{-16}$ comes from wrongly using $B/c$. $\\hat{j}$ lies along the propagation direction, which is not allowed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":131,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a conducting wire carrying a steady direct current, the displacement current is zero.\nReason (R): The electric field inside such a wire does not change with time.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. For a steady current, the electric field driving the charges in the wire is constant in time.\nA is true. Displacement current is $i_d = \\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$. A constant field gives $\\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = 0$, so $i_d = 0$.\nR is exactly why $i_d$ vanishes, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":132,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A person sitting indoors behind closed glass windows on a sunny day does not get a suntan or sunburn.\nReason (R): Ordinary glass absorbs ultraviolet radiation.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Tanning and sunburn are caused by UV exposure, which induces extra melanin production.\nR is true. NCERT states that ordinary glass absorbs UV radiation.\nGlass blocks the UV, so the skin is not exposed to it. R therefore directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":133,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A capacitor has circular plates of radius 5.0 cm. During charging, the uniform electric field between the plates increases at a rate of $6.0 \\times 10^{12}$ V m$^{-1}$ s$^{-1}$. Assume the field is confined to the region between the plates and neglect edge effects. What is the magnitude of the magnetic field at a point in the gap located 10 cm from the axis of the plates? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s.)","image":"","options":["3.33 μT","0.833 μT","1.67 μT","0.417 μT"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: R = 0.05 m, r = 0.10 m (outside the plate edge), $\\frac{dE}{dt} = 6\\times10^{12}$ V m$^{-1}$ s$^{-1}$.\nFormula: Ampere-Maxwell law on a circle of radius r. Electric flux exists only over the plate area $\\pi R^2$, so $B(2\\pi r) = \\mu_0\\varepsilon_0 \\pi R^2 \\frac{dE}{dt}$, giving $B = \\frac{R^2}{2rc^2}\\frac{dE}{dt}$.\nSubstituting: $B = \\frac{(0.05)^2 \\times 6\\times10^{12}}{2\\times0.10\\times9\\times10^{16}} = 8.33\\times10^{-7}$ T.\nSo B = 0.833 μT. Using $r^2$ in place of $R^2$ would wrongly give 3.33 μT.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":134,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"An FM transmitter broadcasts electromagnetic waves of frequency 60 MHz in free space. What is the wavelength of these waves? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s)","image":"","options":["5 m","0.2 m","2 m","50 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\nu = 60 \\times 10^6$ Hz, $c = 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s.\nFormula: $\\nu\\lambda = c$, so $\\lambda = \\frac{c}{\\nu}$.\nSubstituting: $\\lambda = \\frac{3 \\times 10^8}{6 \\times 10^7}$.\nSo $\\lambda = 5$ m. The option 0.2 m comes from inverting the ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":135,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The electric flux through a surface changes at a constant rate of $1.0 \\times 10^{10}$ V m s$^{-1}$. Find the displacement current through the surface. (Take $\\epsilon_0 = 8.85 \\times 10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["88.5 mA","8.85 mA","885 mA","0.885 mA"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = 1.0\\times10^{10}$ V m s$^{-1}$ and $\\epsilon_0 = 8.85\\times10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$.\nFormula: $i_d = \\epsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$\nSubstituting: $i_d = 8.85\\times10^{-12} \\times 10^{10} = 8.85\\times10^{-2}$ A\nSo $i_d$ = 88.5 mA.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":136,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A person sitting in a closed car on a sunny day does not get sunburnt through the window glass. Which property of ordinary glass explains this?","image":"","options":["Glass reflects all visible light","Glass absorbs ultraviolet radiation","Glass absorbs infrared radiation completely","Glass converts ultraviolet into X-rays"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Tanning and sunburn are caused by ultraviolet (UV) radiation.\nAccording to NCERT, ordinary glass absorbs UV radiation, so very little UV passes through a glass window.\nGlass lets most visible light through, so option (1) is wrong.\nInfrared is linked to heating, not tanning, so option (3) does not explain the effect.\nGlass cannot turn UV into X-rays, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":137,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Two beams of electromagnetic radiation each have a wavelength of $10^{-12}$ m in vacuum. Beam P comes from an X-ray tube and beam Q is emitted by a radioactive nucleus. Which statement is correct?","image":"","options":["Both beams must be called gamma rays, because the name depends only on wavelength","Both beams must be called X-rays, because the name depends only on wavelength","P is called an X-ray and Q a gamma ray; they travel at the same speed in vacuum","P is called an X-ray and Q a gamma ray; Q travels faster in vacuum because of its nuclear origin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"There are no sharp boundaries between regions of the spectrum. X-rays (about $10^{-8}$ to $10^{-13}$ m) and gamma rays (about $10^{-10}$ m to below $10^{-14}$ m) overlap.\nIn an overlap region, the classification depends on how the radiation is produced, so options (1) and (2) are wrong.\nRadiation from an X-ray tube is called X-rays, and radiation from radioactive decay is called gamma rays.\nAll electromagnetic waves travel at speed c in vacuum, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":138,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Ultraviolet radiation is used in high-precision applications such as LASIK eye surgery.\nReason (R): Because of its shorter wavelength, ultraviolet radiation can be focussed into very narrow beams.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT mentions LASIK (Laser-assisted in situ keratomileusis) eye surgery as an application of UV.\nR is true: UV has a shorter wavelength than visible light, so it can be focussed into very narrow beams.\nThis narrow focussing is exactly what makes UV suitable for high-precision work, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":139,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The electric field of an electromagnetic wave in vacuum is $E_y = 90\\sin(2.0\\times10^{3}x - 6.0\\times10^{11}t)$ V/m, with $x$ in metres and $t$ in seconds. Which option correctly gives the wavelength, the region of the spectrum, and the magnetic field of this wave?","image":"","options":["$\\lambda \\approx 3.14$ mm, microwave region; $\\vec{B}$ oscillates along the z-axis with amplitude $3\\times10^{-7}$ T","$\\lambda \\approx 3.14$ mm, infrared region; $\\vec{B}$ oscillates along the z-axis with amplitude $3\\times10^{-7}$ T","$\\lambda \\approx 3.14$ mm, microwave region; $\\vec{B}$ oscillates along the x-axis with amplitude $3\\times10^{-7}$ T","$\\lambda \\approx 3.14$ mm, microwave region; $\\vec{B}$ oscillates along the z-axis with amplitude $2.7\\times10^{10}$ T"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Wavelength: $k = 2\\times10^3\\ m^{-1}$, so $\\lambda = 2\\pi/k \\approx 3.14\\times10^{-3}$ m. This lies in the microwave range (0.1 m to 1 mm), not infrared. As a check, $\\nu = \\omega/2\\pi \\approx 95.5$ GHz.\nAmplitude of B: $B_0 = E_0/c = 90/(3\\times10^8) = 3\\times10^{-7}$ T. The value $2.7\\times10^{10}$ T comes from wrongly multiplying by $c$.\nDirection of B: the wave moves along $+x$ and $\\vec{E}$ is along $y$. B must be perpendicular to both, so it lies along $z$, since $\\hat{j}\\times\\hat{k} = \\hat{i}$. B can never be along the direction of propagation ($x$).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":140,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of electromagnetic wave) with List-II (a detector listed for it in NCERT Table 8.1).\nList-I:\n(a) Microwave\n(b) Infrared\n(c) Visible light\n(d) Radio waves\nList-II:\n(i) Receiver's aerials\n(ii) Bolometer\n(iii) Point contact diodes\n(iv) The eye","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Microwaves are detected by point contact diodes, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) Infrared is detected by thermopiles, bolometers and infrared photographic film, so (b)-(ii).\n(c) Visible light is detected by the eye, photocells and photographic film, so (c)-(iv).\n(d) Radio waves are picked up by receiver's aerials, so (d)-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":141,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The electromagnetic nature of light could not be tested by building an ac circuit whose current oscillates at the frequency of yellow light, so the first experimental demonstration of EM waves was made in the radio region.\nReason (R): The frequency of yellow light is about $6 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz, whereas electronic circuits can reach only about $10^{11}$ Hz.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Hertz (1887) demonstrated EM waves at low (radio) frequencies.\nR is true. Yellow light has a frequency of about $6 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz, while circuits reach only about $10^{11}$ Hz.\nR explains A: no circuit can make charges oscillate at optical frequencies, so the test had to be done with radio waves.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":142,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (radiation) with List-II (feature or use):\nList-I\n(A) Ultraviolet\n(B) X-rays\n(C) Gamma rays\n(D) Visible light\nList-II\n(i) Emitted in radioactive decay of nuclei\n(ii) Produced by bombarding a metal target with high-energy electrons\n(iii) Used in lamps to kill germs in water purifiers\n(iv) Detected directly by the human eye\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A–(iii): UV lamps are used to kill germs in water purifiers.\nB–(ii): A common way to generate X-rays is to bombard a metal target with high-energy electrons.\nC–(i): Gamma rays are emitted by radioactive nuclei.\nD–(iv): Visible light is the part of the spectrum detected by the eye.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":143,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Infrared radiation can be detected using thermopiles and bolometers.\nStatement II: Microwaves are detected using Geiger tubes.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Table 8.1 lists thermopiles, bolometers and infrared photographic film as detectors of infrared.\nStatement II is incorrect. Microwaves are detected by point contact diodes.\nGeiger tubes are listed as detectors for X-rays and gamma rays, not for microwaves.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":144,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The electromagnetic waves first produced and detected in the laboratory by Hertz in 1887 had wavelengths of the order of","image":"","options":["a few nanometres","a few micrometres","a few millimetres","a few metres"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT's summary states that Hertz produced EM waves with wavelengths of the order of a few metres. These lie in the radio region.\nOption (3), a few millimetres, is tempting, but that range (25 mm to 5 mm) belongs to J.C. Bose's later, shorter-wavelength waves.\nNanometre and micrometre wavelengths fall in the X-ray/UV and infrared regions, which electronic circuits cannot produce.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":145,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, Maxwell's equations, taken together with which one of the following, mathematically express all the basic laws of electromagnetism?","image":"","options":["Ohm's law","Coulomb's law","Biot-Savart law","Lorentz force formula"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Maxwell's equations tell us how charges and currents produce $\\vec{E}$ and $\\vec{B}$, and how these fields generate each other.\nWe still need a rule for how the fields act on charges. That rule is the Lorentz force, $\\vec{F} = q(\\vec{E} + \\vec{v}\\times\\vec{B})$.\nCoulomb's law and the Biot-Savart law are already contained in Maxwell's equations as special cases.\nOhm's law is an empirical property of materials, not a basic law of electromagnetism.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":146,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) X-rays can be produced by bombarding a metal target with high-energy electrons.\n(ii) X-rays are used to treat certain forms of cancer.\n(iii) Exposure to UV radiation reduces the production of melanin in the skin.\n(iv) Depletion of the ozone layer by CFCs such as freon is a matter of international concern.\n(v) Among UV, X-rays and gamma rays, gamma rays have the longest wavelengths.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is a common way to generate X-rays.\n(ii) Correct: X-rays are used both for diagnosis and as a treatment for some cancers.\n(iii) Incorrect: UV increases melanin production, which causes tanning.\n(iv) Correct: the ozone layer is protective, so its depletion by CFCs is a global concern.\n(v) Incorrect: gamma rays have the shortest wavelengths of the three.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":147,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A capacitor has circular plates of radius 5.0 cm. It is being charged by a constant current of 2.0 A. Using the Ampere-Maxwell law, find the magnitude of the magnetic field between the plates at a distance of 2.0 cm from the axis. (Take $\\mu_0 = 4\\pi \\times 10^{-7}$ T m A$^{-1}$; the electric field between the plates is uniform and fringing is neglected.)","image":"","options":["20 $\\mu$T","8.0 $\\mu$T","3.2 $\\mu$T","1.28 $\\mu$T"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $R = 0.05$ m, $r = 0.02$ m, $i = 2.0$ A. Between the plates $i_c = 0$ and the total $i_d = i$.\nThe loop of radius $r$ encloses only the fraction $r^2/R^2$ of the flux, so the enclosed $i_d = i\\,r^2/R^2$.\nFormula: $B(2\\pi r) = \\mu_0 i\\frac{r^2}{R^2}$, which gives $B = \\frac{\\mu_0 i r}{2\\pi R^2}$\nSubstituting: $B = \\frac{2\\times10^{-7}\\times2\\times0.02}{0.0025} = 3.2\\times10^{-6}$ T\nSo B = 3.2 $\\mu$T. (20 $\\mu$T would wrongly use the full current, as for a straight wire.)\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":148,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The electric field of an electromagnetic wave in a non-magnetic dielectric ($\\mu_r = 1$) is $E_x = 60 \\sin(3z - 6 \\times 10^8 t)$ V/m, where $z$ is in metres and $t$ is in seconds. What is the relative permittivity $\\varepsilon_r$ of the dielectric? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s)","image":"","options":["2.25","1.5","0.44","1.22"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $k = 3$ rad/m, $\\omega = 6 \\times 10^8$ rad/s, $\\mu_r = 1$.\nFormula: The wave speed is $v = \\frac{\\omega}{k}$. Also $v = \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{\\mu_r\\varepsilon_r}}$, so $\\varepsilon_r = \\left(\\frac{c}{v}\\right)^2$.\nSubstituting: $v = \\frac{6 \\times 10^8}{3} = 2 \\times 10^8$ m/s. The refractive index is $n = \\frac{c}{v} = 1.5$.\nSo $\\varepsilon_r = n^2 = 2.25$. (1.5 is the refractive index, not $\\varepsilon_r$.)\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":149,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A parallel plate capacitor is being charged. At an instant, the electric flux through the gap between its plates is increasing at the rate $\\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = 2.26 \\times 10^{11}$ V m/s. A circular loop of radius 4.0 cm is centred on the straight lead wire, just outside the capacitor. A tiffin-shaped surface has this loop as its rim, and its flat bottom lies between the plates, intercepting all of this flux. Using the generalised (Ampere-Maxwell) law, find the magnitude of the magnetic field on the loop. Take $\\varepsilon_0 = 8.85 \\times 10^{-12}$ C$^2$ N$^{-1}$ m$^{-2}$ and $\\mu_0 = 4\\pi \\times 10^{-7}$ T m/A.","image":"","options":["10 μT","5 μT","0 μT","20 μT"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt} = 2.26\\times10^{11}$ V m/s, $r = 0.04$ m. No conduction current crosses the tiffin surface.\nFormula: $i_d = \\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$ and $B(2\\pi r) = \\mu_0 i_d$.\nSubstituting: $i_d = 8.85\\times10^{-12} \\times 2.26\\times10^{11} \\approx 2.0$ A.\nThen $B = \\frac{4\\pi\\times10^{-7}\\times 2.0}{2\\pi \\times 0.04} = 1.0\\times10^{-5}$ T.\nThe value 0 is what the old law (without the missing term) wrongly predicts for this surface.\nSo B = 10 μT.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":150,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A parallel plate capacitor is being charged by a time-dependent current $i(t)$. A circular loop around the connecting wire forms the rim of a pot-shaped surface whose bottom lies between the capacitor plates and nowhere touches the wire. What conduction current crosses this pot-shaped surface?","image":"","options":["$i(t)$","Zero","$\\frac{i(t)}{2}$","$2i(t)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The pot-shaped surface never touches the wire. Its bottom lies in the gap between the plates.\nNo charges flow across the gap, so no conduction current crosses any part of this surface.\nThe original Ampere's law therefore gives zero on the right-hand side for this surface.\nOption (1), $i(t)$, applies only to a flat surface that is pierced by the wire.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":151,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A plane electromagnetic wave travels in vacuum along the +z direction. At a certain point and instant, its electric field points along +x. Along which direction does the magnetic field point at that point and instant?","image":"","options":["+y direction","−y direction","+z direction","−x direction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In an EM wave, $\\vec{E}$, $\\vec{B}$ and the propagation direction are mutually perpendicular.\nThe direction of $\\vec{E}\\times\\vec{B}$ gives the direction of propagation.\nWe need $\\hat{i}\\times\\hat{B} = \\hat{k}$. Since $\\hat{i}\\times\\hat{j} = \\hat{k}$, $\\vec{B}$ is along +y.\n−y would give propagation along −z. +z and −x are not perpendicular to both $\\vec{E}$ and the propagation direction.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":152,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Match the Maxwell's equations in vacuum (List-I) with their physical meaning (List-II).\nList-I\n(A) $\\oint \\vec{E}\\cdot d\\vec{A} = \\frac{Q}{\\varepsilon_0}$\n(B) $\\oint \\vec{B}\\cdot d\\vec{A} = 0$\n(C) $\\oint \\vec{E}\\cdot d\\vec{l} = -\\frac{d\\Phi_B}{dt}$\n(D) $\\oint \\vec{B}\\cdot d\\vec{l} = \\mu_0 i_c + \\mu_0\\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$\nList-II\n(I) A magnetic field changing with time produces an electric field\n(II) Electric charges are the sources of electric field\n(III) Both conduction current and changing electric flux produce magnetic field\n(IV) Isolated magnetic poles do not exist","image":"","options":["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A) Gauss's law for electricity links electric flux to enclosed charge, so charge is the source of E: II.\n(B) Net magnetic flux through any closed surface is zero, so there are no monopoles: IV.\n(C) Faraday's law says a changing magnetic flux gives an emf, and hence an electric field: I.\n(D) The Ampere-Maxwell law makes $i_c$ and $\\varepsilon_0 \\frac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$ the sources of B: III.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":153,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A radio wave of frequency 100 MHz enters a non-magnetic medium ($\\mu_r = 1$) of relative permittivity $\\varepsilon_r = 2.25$. What is the wavelength of the wave inside the medium? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s)","image":"","options":["2 m","3 m","1.33 m","4.5 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\nu = 10^8$ Hz, $\\varepsilon_r = 2.25$, $\\mu_r = 1$.\nFormula: $v = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu\\varepsilon}} = \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{\\mu_r \\varepsilon_r}}$, and $\\lambda = \\frac{v}{\\nu}$. The frequency does not change on entering the medium.\nSubstituting: $v = \\frac{3 \\times 10^8}{1.5} = 2 \\times 10^8$ m/s, so $\\lambda = \\frac{2 \\times 10^8}{10^8}$.\nSo $\\lambda = 2$ m. (3 m is the wavelength in vacuum.)\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":154,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The magnetic field of an electromagnetic wave in vacuum is $B_z = B_0\\sin(kx - 6\\pi \\times 10^{9}\\,t)$, where t is in seconds. What is the frequency of the wave?","image":"","options":["3 GHz","6 GHz","18.85 GHz","1.5 GHz"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the coefficient of t is the angular frequency, $\\omega = 6\\pi \\times 10^{9}$ rad/s.\nFormula: $\\nu = \\frac{\\omega}{2\\pi}$.\nSubstituting: $\\nu = \\frac{6\\pi \\times 10^{9}}{2\\pi} = 3 \\times 10^{9}$ Hz.\nSo $\\nu$ = 3 GHz. Reading 6 GHz confuses $\\omega$ with $\\nu$, and 18.85 GHz is just $6\\pi \\times 10^9$ taken as a frequency.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":155,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The electric field of a plane electromagnetic wave in vacuum is $E_x = E_0 \\sin(kz - \\omega t)$, with $k = \\pi$ rad/m. What is the frequency of the wave? (Take $c = 3\\times10^8$ m/s)","image":"","options":["150 MHz","300 MHz","47.7 MHz","942 MHz"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $k = \\pi$ rad/m, $c = 3\\times10^8$ m/s\nFormula: $\\omega = ck$ and $\\nu = \\frac{\\omega}{2\\pi} = \\frac{ck}{2\\pi}$\nSubstituting: $\\nu = \\frac{3\\times10^8 \\times \\pi}{2\\pi} = 1.5\\times10^8$ Hz\nSo $\\nu$ = 150 MHz. The option 942 MHz equals $ck$; it comes from forgetting to divide by $2\\pi$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":156,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"In vacuum, Gauss's law for magnetism is $\\oint \\vec{B}\\cdot d\\vec{A} = 0$ for every closed surface. What does this equation physically convey?","image":"","options":["The magnetic field is zero at every point of any closed surface","No isolated magnetic poles (magnetic monopoles) exist","A changing magnetic field always produces an electric field","Magnetic field lines start on north poles and end on south poles"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Gauss's law for electricity, $\\oint \\vec{E}\\cdot d\\vec{A} = Q/\\epsilon_0$, has electric charge as the source of flux.\nFor magnetism the right side is zero, so the net magnetic flux through any closed surface is zero. There is no magnetic 'charge', that is, no monopole.\nOption (1) is wrong because the net flux can be zero even when $B$ is not zero, since inward and outward flux cancel.\nOption (4) fails because magnetic field lines form closed loops and have no starting or ending points.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":157,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A parallel plate capacitor has circular plates of radius 10 cm. It is being charged by a constant current of 2 A in the connecting wires. Assume the electric field between the plates is uniform and confined to the region between them. What is the magnitude of the magnetic field at a point between the plates, 5 cm from the central axis? (Take $\\mu_0 = 4\\pi \\times 10^{-7}$ T m/A)","image":"","options":["2.0 $\\mu$T","4.0 $\\mu$T","8.0 $\\mu$T","1.0 $\\mu$T"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $i = 2$ A, $R = 0.10$ m, $r = 0.05$ m. Between the plates, $i_d = i$, spread uniformly over the plate area.\nThe displacement current enclosed by a loop of radius r is $i_d' = i\\frac{r^2}{R^2}$.\nFormula: by the Ampere-Maxwell law, $B(2\\pi r) = \\mu_0 i \\frac{r^2}{R^2}$, so $B = \\frac{\\mu_0 i r}{2\\pi R^2}$.\nSubstituting: $B = \\frac{2 \\times 10^{-7} \\times 2 \\times 0.05}{0.01} = 2 \\times 10^{-6}$ T.\nSo B = 2.0 $\\mu$T. The value 4.0 $\\mu$T wrongly uses the full current, which applies only at the edge, r = R.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":158,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The original form of Ampere's circuital law, $\\oint \\vec{B}\\cdot d\\vec{l} = \\mu_0 i$, gives two different values of B at a point outside a charging capacitor.\nReason (R): The value of $\\oint \\vec{B}\\cdot d\\vec{l}$ around a given loop depends on which surface bounded by that loop is chosen.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. A flat surface pierced by the wire gives $B = \\frac{\\mu_0 i}{2\\pi r}$, while a pot-shaped surface passing between the plates gives B = 0.\nR is false. The line integral depends only on the loop itself.\nThe surface-dependent part is the current on the right-hand side, which is what the missing term had to fix.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":159,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"According to Table 8.1, the ultraviolet and X-ray regions meet near a wavelength of 1 nm. What is the energy of a photon of wavelength 1 nm, in eV?\nTake $h = 6.63\\times10^{-34}$ J s, $c = 3\\times10^{8}$ m/s and $1\\text{ eV} = 1.6\\times10^{-19}$ J.","image":"","options":["$1.24\\times10^{3}$ eV","$1.24\\times10^{2}$ eV","12.4 eV","$1.24\\times10^{4}$ eV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\lambda = 1\\times10^{-9}$ m, $h = 6.63\\times10^{-34}$ J s, $c = 3\\times10^{8}$ m/s.\nFormula: $E = h\\nu = \\frac{hc}{\\lambda}$\nSubstituting: $E = \\frac{6.63\\times10^{-34}\\times3\\times10^{8}}{10^{-9}} = 1.99\\times10^{-16}$ J.\nConverting to eV: $\\frac{1.99\\times10^{-16}}{1.6\\times10^{-19}} \\approx 1243$ eV.\nSo E ≈ $1.24\\times10^{3}$ eV, about 1.24 keV, which is typical of soft X-rays.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":160,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: An electric charge oscillating harmonically with frequency $\\nu$ produces electromagnetic waves of the same frequency $\\nu$.\nStatement II: The wavelength of the radiated wave depends on the amplitude of oscillation of the charge.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. NCERT says the frequency of the EM wave equals the frequency of oscillation of the charge.\nStatement II is false. In vacuum $\\lambda = \\frac{c}{\\nu}$, so the wavelength is fixed by the frequency alone.\nThe amplitude of the charge's motion affects how strong the radiation is, not its wavelength.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":161,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A parallel plate capacitor is being charged by a time-varying current $i(t)$. A circular loop of radius $r$ surrounds the connecting wire just outside the capacitor. Ampere's circuital law in its original form, $\\oint \\vec{B}\\cdot d\\vec{l} = \\mu_0 i$, is applied using a pot-shaped surface. The rim of this surface is the same loop, and its bottom lies between the capacitor plates. Why does the right-hand side come out as zero for this surface?","image":"","options":["The electric field between the capacitor plates is zero while the capacitor is charging","No conduction current crosses the pot-shaped surface anywhere","The magnetic field on the circular rim is zero for this choice of surface","The pot-shaped surface does not have the same boundary as the flat circular surface"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: the original law uses only the conduction current that crosses the chosen surface.\nThe pot-shaped surface never touches the wire. Its bottom lies in the gap between the plates, where no charges flow. So the conduction current through it is zero.\nOption (1) is wrong: there is a growing electric field between the plates.\nOption (3) is wrong: the rim is the same loop, so the left-hand side $B(2\\pi r)$ is unchanged.\nOption (4) is wrong: both surfaces share the same rim. That shared rim is exactly why the result is a contradiction.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":162,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A plane electromagnetic wave travels in vacuum along the +x direction. At a certain point and instant, its electric field is $9\\,\\hat{k}$ V/m. What is the magnetic field at that point and instant? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s)","image":"","options":["$3 \\times 10^{-8}\\,\\hat{j}$ T","$-3 \\times 10^{-8}\\,\\hat{j}$ T","$-2.7 \\times 10^{9}\\,\\hat{j}$ T","$3 \\times 10^{-8}\\,\\hat{k}$ T"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Magnitude: $B = E/c = 9/(3 \\times 10^{8}) = 3 \\times 10^{-8}$ T.\nDirection: B must be perpendicular to both E (along z) and the propagation direction (along x), so it lies along $\\pm y$.\nAlso, $\\vec{E} \\times \\vec{B}$ must point along +x. Since $\\hat{k} \\times (-\\hat{j}) = \\hat{i}$, B points along $-\\hat{j}$.\nOption (3) wrongly multiplies E by c, and option (4) makes B parallel to E.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":163,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Welders' goggles with glass windows\n(B) LASIK eye surgery\n(C) Gamma rays\n(D) Ozone layer at about 40–50 km altitude\nList-II\n(i) Short-wavelength UV focussed into very narrow beams\n(ii) Glass absorbs the large amount of UV from the welding arc\n(iii) Emitted by radioactive nuclei and in nuclear reactions\n(iv) Absorbs most of the ultraviolet light coming from the sun\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A–(ii): Welding arcs produce a lot of UV. The glass in goggles and masks absorbs it and protects the eyes.\nB–(i): UV has a short wavelength, so it can be focussed into narrow beams for precise surgery such as LASIK.\nC–(iii): Gamma rays come from radioactive nuclei and nuclear reactions.\nD–(iv): Ozone at 40–50 km absorbs most of the sun's UV.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":164,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following is NOT used to produce microwaves?","image":"","options":["Klystron","Magnetron","Gunn diode","Thermopile"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT: microwaves are produced by special vacuum tubes called klystrons and magnetrons, and by Gunn diodes.\nA thermopile does not produce microwaves.\nIt is a detector of infrared radiation (Table 8.1), so it is the odd one out.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":165,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of electromagnetic wave) with List-II (a method of its production or detection):\nList-I\n(A) Radio waves\n(B) Microwaves\n(C) Infrared waves\n(D) Gamma rays\nList-II\n(I) Bolometer\n(II) Radioactive decay of the nucleus\n(III) Receiver's aerials\n(IV) Magnetron valve","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Radio waves are detected by receiver's aerials, so A-III.\n(B) Microwaves are produced by klystron or magnetron valves, so B-IV.\n(C) Infrared waves are detected by thermopiles, bolometers and infrared film, so C-I.\n(D) Gamma rays come from radioactive decay of the nucleus, so D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":166,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (quantities for a plane EM wave in vacuum) with List-II (expressions):\nList-I\n(A) Magnitude of wave vector, $k$\n(B) Angular frequency, $\\omega$\n(C) Speed of propagation, $\\frac{\\omega}{k}$\n(D) Frequency, $\\nu$\nList-II\n(I) $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0 \\varepsilon_0}}$\n(II) $\\frac{c}{\\lambda}$\n(III) $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\lambda}$\n(IV) $2\\pi\\nu$","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $k = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\lambda}$, so A matches III.\n(B) $\\omega = 2\\pi\\nu$, so B matches IV.\n(C) $\\frac{\\omega}{k} = c = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0}}$, so C matches I.\n(D) From $\\nu\\lambda = c$, $\\nu = \\frac{c}{\\lambda}$, so D matches II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":167,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"An electromagnetic wave of frequency 100 MHz enters a non-magnetic medium ($\\mu_r = 1$) of relative permittivity $\\varepsilon_r = 4$. What is its wavelength inside the medium? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s)","image":"","options":["1.5 m","3.0 m","0.75 m","6.0 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\nu = 10^{8}$ Hz, $\\varepsilon = 4\\varepsilon_0$, $\\mu = \\mu_0$.\nFormula: $v = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu\\varepsilon}} = \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{\\mu_r \\varepsilon_r}}$, and $\\lambda = v/\\nu$. The frequency does not change in the medium.\nSubstituting: $v = 3 \\times 10^{8}/2 = 1.5 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s, so $\\lambda = 1.5 \\times 10^{8}/10^{8}$.\nSo $\\lambda$ = 1.5 m. The value 3.0 m is the wavelength in vacuum.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":168,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"A photon has an energy of 12.4 keV. What is its wavelength, and to which region of the electromagnetic spectrum (as per NCERT Table 8.1) does it belong? (Take $hc \\approx 1240$ eV nm)","image":"","options":["0.1 nm; X-rays","10 nm; ultraviolet","100 nm; ultraviolet","1000 nm; infrared"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $E = 12.4$ keV $= 12400$ eV and $hc = 1240$ eV nm.\nFormula: $\\lambda = \\frac{hc}{E}$.\nSubstituting: $\\lambda = \\frac{1240}{12400} = 0.1$ nm.\nTable 8.1 gives X-rays from 1 nm down to $10^{-3}$ nm, and 0.1 nm falls inside this range.\nSo the photon is an X-ray photon of wavelength 0.1 nm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":169,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following is a source of electromagnetic waves?","image":"","options":["A point charge held at rest","A charge moving with constant velocity along a straight line","A charge oscillating to and fro about a mean position","A steady direct current flowing in a long straight wire"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Maxwell's theory shows that only accelerated charges radiate electromagnetic waves.\nA charge at rest produces only an electrostatic field.\nUniform motion, as in a steady current, gives a magnetic field that does not change with time, so no wave is produced.\nAn oscillating charge keeps changing its velocity, so it is accelerated. It sets up oscillating E and B fields that regenerate each other and travel outward.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":170,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a plane electromagnetic wave in vacuum, the average energy density of the electric field equals the average energy density of the magnetic field.\nStatement II: Since $B_0 = \\frac{E_0}{c}$ and $c$ is very large, the magnetic field carries only a negligible fraction of the energy of the wave.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: Average electric energy density is $\\frac{1}{4}\\varepsilon_0 E_0^2$. Average magnetic energy density is $\\frac{B_0^2}{4\\mu_0} = \\frac{E_0^2}{4\\mu_0 c^2}$.\nUsing $c^2 = \\frac{1}{\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0}$, the magnetic term also becomes $\\frac{1}{4}\\varepsilon_0 E_0^2$. So the two are equal, and Statement I is correct.\nStatement II: $B_0$ is numerically small, but the energy density depends on $\\frac{B^2}{2\\mu_0}$, and $\\mu_0$ is also small. The magnetic field carries half the energy, so Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-171","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":171,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The magnetic field of a plane electromagnetic wave in vacuum is $B_x = 3.0\\times10^{-7}\\sin(2.0\\times10^{3}\\,y - 6.0\\times10^{11}\\,t)$ T. The wave travels along the +y direction. Which expression correctly gives the electric field of this wave? (Take $c = 3\\times10^{8}$ m/s.)","image":"","options":["$E_z = 90\\sin(2.0\\times10^{3}\\,y - 6.0\\times10^{11}\\,t)$ V/m","$E_y = 90\\sin(2.0\\times10^{3}\\,y - 6.0\\times10^{11}\\,t)$ V/m","$E_z = 1.0\\times10^{-15}\\sin(2.0\\times10^{3}\\,y - 6.0\\times10^{11}\\,t)$ V/m","$E_x = 90\\sin(2.0\\times10^{3}\\,y - 6.0\\times10^{11}\\,t)$ V/m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Find the amplitude. $E_0 = cB_0 = 3\\times10^{8}\\times3.0\\times10^{-7} = 90$ V/m. Option (3) wrongly divides $B_0$ by c instead of multiplying.\nStep 2: Find the axis. E must be perpendicular to B (the x-axis) and to the propagation direction (the y-axis). So E lies along the z-axis. This rules out $E_y$ (along propagation) and $E_x$ (parallel to B).\nStep 3: Check the direction. $\\hat{k}\\times\\hat{i} = \\hat{j}$, so $\\vec{E}\\times\\vec{B}$ points along +y, as required.\nStep 4: The phase $(ky - \\omega t)$ stays the same, with $\\frac{\\omega}{k} = 3\\times10^{8}$ m/s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-172","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":172,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"The FM (frequency modulated) radio band extends over which frequency range?","image":"","options":["530 kHz to 1710 kHz","54 MHz to 890 MHz","88 MHz to 108 MHz","500 kHz to 54 MHz"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT: the FM radio band extends from 88 MHz to 108 MHz.\n530–1710 kHz is the AM band, so (1) is wrong.\n54–890 MHz is the TV band, so (2) is wrong.\nShort-wave bands use frequencies up to 54 MHz, so (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-173","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":173,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Gamma radiation of wavelength $10^{-14}$ m to $10^{-15}$ m typically originates from an atomic nucleus.\n(ii) A transmitting antenna radiates most efficiently at wavelengths of about its own size. So an FM station broadcasting at 100 MHz needs an antenna of the order of 3 m.\n(iii) Visible light emitted by atoms has a wavelength comparable to the size of an atom.\n(iv) The boundaries between regions of the electromagnetic spectrum are sharply defined by wavelength.\n(v) Radio waves are produced by accelerating electrons in a circuit.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT links $\\gamma$-rays of this wavelength to nuclear sources.\n(ii) Correct: $\\lambda = \\frac{c}{\\nu} = \\frac{3\\times10^{8}}{10^{8}} = 3$ m, so the antenna is of the order of 3 m.\n(iii) Incorrect: visible light (400–700 nm) is much longer than atomic size (about 0.1 nm).\n(iv) Incorrect: there are no sharp divisions, and the regions overlap.\n(v) Correct: as stated in NCERT.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-EM-WAVES-174","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electromagnetic Waves","qno":174,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electromagnetic Waves","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Infrared waves are called heat waves because water molecules readily absorb them and their thermal motion increases.\n(ii) The infrared band lies adjacent to the high-frequency end of the visible spectrum.\n(iii) In the greenhouse effect, the earth's surface absorbs visible light and re-radiates it as longer-wavelength infrared, which is trapped by carbon dioxide and water vapour.\n(iv) Thermopiles and bolometers are used to detect infrared radiation.\n(v) Semiconductor light emitting diodes used in TV remote switches emit infrared.\n(vi) Microwaves suit radar systems because of their long wavelengths compared with radio waves.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: water molecules absorb infrared, their thermal motion increases, and they heat up.\n(ii) Incorrect: infrared lies next to the low-frequency (long-wavelength) end of the visible spectrum.\n(iii) Correct: this is how NCERT describes the greenhouse effect.\n(iv) Correct: Table 8.1 lists thermopiles and bolometers as infrared detectors.\n(v) Correct: LEDs in remote switches emit infrared.\n(vi) Incorrect: microwaves suit radar because of their short wavelengths.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 8 · Electromagnetic Waves","difficulty":"hard"}]});
